(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("platform",{"platform/what-platform-engineering-is-and-how-it-differs-from-devops-and-sre":{"html":"\u003cp>Every product team needs the same things to ship software: a repository, a pipeline, somewhere to run, secrets, logs, alerts and a way to ask for a database. If each team builds these alone, the company pays for the same work many times and gets many slightly different, slightly broken results. Platform engineering is the answer to that waste: one team builds the common parts once, and makes them easy to use.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a platform is and what it is not\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How platform engineering relates to DevOps and SRE\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The problems it solves and the ones it creates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The signs that a company is ready for a platform team\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-platform-in-plain-words\">A platform, in plain words\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The CNCF platforms white paper describes a platform as an integrated collection of capabilities, presented according to the needs of its users. The important words are &quot;integrated&quot; and &quot;users&quot;. A pile of tools is not a platform. A platform is a set of tools, services and documentation that fit together, with a clear front door, built for internal customers: the developers who build and run the company&#39;s products.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A platform is not a rebrand of the operations team, a single product such as Kubernetes, or a mandate that everyone must use one stack. If developers must file tickets and wait, you have a service desk. If they can get what they need in minutes without knowing how it works underneath, you have a platform.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-it-relates-to-devops-and-sre\">How it relates to DevOps and SRE\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>DevOps is a culture and a set of practices: shared ownership of delivery and operation, automation, fast feedback. SRE applies software engineering to reliability, with service level objectives, error budgets and a focus on toil. Platform engineering is a way of organising the work so that these practices are easy to follow at scale.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Main question\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Typical output\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>DevOps\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>How do we build and run software together?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Shared practices, pipelines, ownership\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>SRE\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>How reliable should this service be, and how do we keep it so?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>SLOs, error budgets, incident process\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Platform engineering\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>How do we make the right way the easy way for every team?\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Self-service tools, golden paths, a portal\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The three overlap. A platform team often includes people with SRE skills, and a good platform bakes in SLO dashboards and safe delivery. The difference is the customer: an SRE team is usually attached to services, while a platform team serves other engineers.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-problem-it-solves-cognitive-load\">The problem it solves: cognitive load\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>When &quot;you build it, you run it&quot; meets a modern cloud stack, a product developer is asked to know Kubernetes, Terraform, IAM, networking, observability, security scanning and the pipeline, on top of their own domain. The mental effort is called cognitive load. Teams slow down, copy-paste configuration they do not understand and make unsafe choices. A platform removes the undifferentiated parts of that load so developers can spend attention on the product.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-problems-it-creates\">The problems it creates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Platforms have failure modes of their own. A team that builds what it finds interesting, not what users need, ships a platform nobody adopts. A platform that is mandatory but poor becomes a bottleneck. A platform that hides everything leaves developers helpless during an incident. Later lessons deal with each: treating the platform as a product, golden paths that can be left, and measurable adoption.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"signs-you-are-ready\">Signs you are ready\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A platform team makes sense when several product teams repeat the same setup work, new engineers need weeks to deploy for the first time, security and compliance checks differ from team to team, or the operations team spends most of its time on repetitive requests. In a company with two teams and one service, a shared repository of templates is enough, and a dedicated platform team would be a cost without a customer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-small-thinking-tool\">A small thinking tool\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Before building anything, write down the customer, the job and the current cost. The snippet below turns a few observations into a rough yearly estimate of wasted effort, which is the kind of number a platform proposal needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"teams = 12\nsetups_per_team_per_year = 3\nhours_per_setup = 16\nhourly_cost = 70\n\nhours = teams * setups_per_team_per_year * hours_per_setup\nprint(&quot;hours repeated each year:&quot;, hours)\nprint(&quot;rough cost:&quot;, hours * hourly_cost)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">teams = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">12\u003c/span>\nsetups_per_team_per_year = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>\nhours_per_setup = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">16\u003c/span>\nhourly_cost = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">70\u003c/span>\n\nhours = teams * setups_per_team_per_year * hours_per_setup\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;hours repeated each year:&quot;\u003c/span>, hours)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rough cost:&quot;\u003c/span>, hours * hourly_cost)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The numbers are invented; use your own. The point is to size the repeated work before proposing a team to remove it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"4\" data-edges=\"dev&gt;front&gt;self-service;front&gt;plat;plat&gt;infra;team&gt;plat&gt;owns;dev&gt;team&gt;feedback\" style=\"--rows:4\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Where a platform sits\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"87.5\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"87.5\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dev\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Product teams\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"front\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Front door\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"plat\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Platform capabilities\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"infra\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cloud and clusters\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"team\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:87.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Platform team\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Product teams to Front door (self-service); 2, Front door to Platform capabilities; 3, Platform capabilities to Cloud and clusters; 4, Platform team to Platform capabilities (owns); 5, Product teams to Platform team (feedback).\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dev\">\u003cdt>Product teams \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Build and run their own services.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Overloaded with infrastructure detail.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"front\">\u003cdt>Front door \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Portal, CLI and templates.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unclear or hidden entry points.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"plat\">\u003cdt>Platform capabilities \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Delivery, runtime, data, secrets and observability.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A pile of tools that do not fit together.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"infra\">\u003cdt>Cloud and clusters \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The infrastructure underneath.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Details leaking up to users.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"team\">\u003cdt>Platform team \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Builds, supports and measures the platform.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Behaves like a ticket queue.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-platform-nobody-used\">Incident: The platform nobody used\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A company built an internal platform over a year. Six months after launch, only two of fourteen teams used it, and the rest kept their own pipelines.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"platformctl adoption --by team\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>platformctl adoption --by team\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">team          uses-platform   last-deploy-via\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">payments      no              own-pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">search        no              own-pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">checkout      yes             platform\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">identity      no              own-pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team had designed the platform around its own preferences and had never interviewed the product teams. Most teams found the first deploy slower than their current pipeline. The platform team then spent a month with three product teams, found that the slowest step was getting a database, and rebuilt the front door around that. Adoption rose because the platform now removed a pain people already had.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Renaming the operations team and changing nothing else\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building tools because they are interesting, not because users need them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making the platform mandatory before it is good\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hiding so much that developers cannot debug at 3am\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Starting a platform team when there is no repeated work to remove\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"a-platform-in-plain-words","text":"A platform, in plain words"},{"id":"how-it-relates-to-devops-and-sre","text":"How it relates to DevOps and SRE"},{"id":"the-problem-it-solves-cognitive-load","text":"The problem it solves: cognitive load"},{"id":"the-problems-it-creates","text":"The problems it creates"},{"id":"signs-you-are-ready","text":"Signs you are ready"},{"id":"a-small-thinking-tool","text":"A small thinking tool"},{"id":"incident-the-platform-nobody-used","text":"Incident: The platform nobody used"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"CNCF platforms white paper","u":"https://tag-app-delivery.cncf.io/whitepapers/platforms/"},{"t":"DORA research programme","u":"https://dora.dev/"}],"text":"Every product team needs the same things to ship software: a repository, a pipeline, somewhere to run, secrets, logs, alerts and a way to ask for a database. If each team builds these alone, the company pays for the same work many times and gets many slightly different, slightly broken results. Platform engineering is the answer to that waste: one team builds the common parts once, and makes them easy to use. What you will learn What a platform is and what it is not How platform engineering relates to DevOps and SRE The problems it solves and the ones it creates The signs that a company is ready for a platform team A platform, in plain words The CNCF platforms white paper describes a platform as an integrated collection of capabilities, presented according to the needs of its users. The important words are \"integrated\" and \"users\". A pile of tools is not a platform. A platform is a set of tools, services and documentation that fit together, with a clear front door, built for internal customers: the developers who build and run the company&#39;s products. A platform is not a rebrand of the operations team, a single product such as Kubernetes, or a mandate that everyone must use one stack. If developers must file tickets and wait, you have a service desk. If they can get what they need in minutes without knowing how it works underneath, you have a platform. How it relates to DevOps and SRE DevOps is a culture and a set of practices: shared ownership of delivery and operation, automation, fast feedback. SRE applies software engineering to reliability, with service level objectives, error budgets and a focus on toil. Platform engineering is a way of organising the work so that these practices are easy to follow at scale. Main question Typical output DevOps How do we build and run software together? Shared practices, pipelines, ownership SRE How reliable should this service be, and how do we keep it so? SLOs, error budgets, incident process Platform engineering How do we make the right way the easy way for every team? Self-service tools, golden paths, a portal The three overlap. A platform team often includes people with SRE skills, and a good platform bakes in SLO dashboards and safe delivery. The difference is the customer: an SRE team is usually attached to services, while a platform team serves other engineers. The problem it solves: cognitive load When \"you build it, you run it\" meets a modern cloud stack, a product developer is asked to know Kubernetes, Terraform, IAM, networking, observability, security scanning and the pipeline, on top of their own domain. The mental effort is called cognitive load. Teams slow down, copy-paste configuration they do not understand and make unsafe choices. A platform removes the undifferentiated parts of that load so developers can spend attention on the product. The problems it creates Platforms have failure modes of their own. A team that builds what it finds interesting, not what users need, ships a platform nobody adopts. A platform that is mandatory but poor becomes a bottleneck. A platform that hides everything leaves developers helpless during an incident. Later lessons deal with each: treating the platform as a product, golden paths that can be left, and measurable adoption. Signs you are ready A platform team makes sense when several product teams repeat the same setup work, new engineers need weeks to deploy for the first time, security and compliance checks differ from team to team, or the operations team spends most of its time on repetitive requests. In a company with two teams and one service, a shared repository of templates is enough, and a dedicated platform team would be a cost without a customer. A small thinking tool Before building anything, write down the customer, the job and the current cost. The snippet below turns a few observations into a rough yearly estimate of wasted effort, which is the kind of number a platform proposal needs. teams = 12 setups_per_team_per_yea"},"platform/platform-as-a-product-users-roadmap-and-feedback":{"html":"\u003cp>The most common reason a platform fails is not technical. It is that nobody treated it as something people have to want. Internal users usually have a choice, even when it is unofficial: they can keep their own scripts, buy a managed service or ignore the platform and ask a friendly engineer for help. A platform that wins has to be better than those alternatives.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How to identify users and their jobs\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to find the pain worth fixing first\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The idea of the thinnest viable platform\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to run a roadmap, feedback and release notes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-users-not-tools\">Start with users, not tools\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>List the groups who use or are affected by the platform: product developers, data engineers, security reviewers, new joiners, on-call engineers. For each, write the jobs they are trying to get done: &quot;deploy a change to production&quot;, &quot;get a database for a prototype&quot;, &quot;see why my service is slow&quot;. Interview a handful of them and watch them work. You will find friction that no survey would reveal, such as a copied command that everyone has to edit by hand.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A useful habit is to record each interview as a short journey: step, time taken, who helped, what went wrong. Add the steps up. The slowest and most painful steps across many teams are candidates for the first release.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rank-by-pain-and-reach\">Rank by pain and reach\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For each problem, estimate how many teams have it, how often, and how much time it costs. Multiply roughly and sort. This avoids two common errors: fixing the loudest complaint, and fixing the most interesting problem. The ranking also gives you a defensible answer when someone asks why their request is not first.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"problems = [\n    (&quot;get a database&quot;, 11, 6, 4.0),\n    (&quot;add a pipeline&quot;, 12, 3, 6.0),\n    (&quot;rotate a secret&quot;, 8, 12, 0.5),\n    (&quot;create a dashboard&quot;, 9, 2, 2.0),\n]\n# teams affected, times per year, hours each time\nranked = sorted(problems, key=lambda p: p[1] * p[2] * p[3], reverse=True)\nfor name, teams, times, hours in ranked:\n    print(f&quot;{name:20} {teams * times * hours:6.0f} hours per year&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">problems = [\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;get a database&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">11\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">6\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4.0\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;add a pipeline&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">12\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">6.0\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;rotate a secret&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">12\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.5\u003c/span>),\n    (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;create a dashboard&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">9\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2.0\u003c/span>),\n]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># teams affected, times per year, hours each time\u003c/span>\nranked = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sorted\u003c/span>(problems, key=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">lambda\u003c/span> p: p[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>] * p[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>] * p[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>], reverse=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> name, teams, times, hours \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> ranked:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{name:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">20\u003c/span>}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{teams * times * hours:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">6.0\u003c/span>f}\u003c/span> hours per year&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-thinnest-viable-platform\">The thinnest viable platform\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Team Topologies uses the phrase &quot;thinnest viable platform&quot; for the smallest set of capabilities that lets product teams move faster. It might be a documented wiki page at first, then a template, then a self-service workflow. Each step earns the next. Avoid the temptation to build the full architecture diagram before anyone has used a single piece.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"make-adoption-a-choice\">Make adoption a choice\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Mandates give you usage but hide quality. If you can, let teams opt in, and watch what they choose. A rising share of new services created through the platform, and teams coming back for the second and third thing, are better signals than a mandate. Where a mandate is justified, for example a security control, make it narrow and explain why.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-roadmap-people-can-read\">A roadmap people can read\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Publish a short roadmap with three horizons: now, next and later. State the problem each item solves and the users it helps, not only the technology. Say what you are not doing and why. Keep a visible changelog with release notes written for users: what changed, what they need to do and what they gain.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"feedback-loops\">Feedback loops\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Collect feedback in more than one way: an office hour, a channel with an on-call rotation for questions, a short survey after common tasks, and direct interviews each quarter. Close the loop. When someone reports a problem, tell them what happened. A platform team that never answers will soon stop being told.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"name-an-owner-and-a-customer-for-each-capability\">Name an owner and a customer for each capability\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Each capability needs a product owner on the platform side and, ideally, a named pilot customer on the product side. The pilot customer tells you whether the capability solves a real job, and becomes a reference for others.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"users&gt;pain;pain&gt;build;build&gt;ship;ship&gt;measure;measure&gt;users&gt;next round\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The platform product loop\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"90.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"10.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"users\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Users and jobs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pain\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Ranked pain\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"build\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Thinnest slice\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ship\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Release and notes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"measure\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Adoption and feedback\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Users and jobs to Ranked pain; 2, Ranked pain to Thinnest slice; 3, Thinnest slice to Release and notes; 4, Release and notes to Adoption and feedback; 5, Adoption and feedback to Users and jobs (next round).\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"users\">\u003cdt>Users and jobs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Interviews and observation of real work.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Assumptions instead of watching users.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pain\">\u003cdt>Ranked pain \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reach times frequency times cost.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> The loudest request wins by default.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"build\">\u003cdt>Thinnest slice \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> The smallest capability that removes the pain.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A big design before first use.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ship\">\u003cdt>Release and notes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Docs, a changelog and an announcement.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Users never hear about it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"measure\">\u003cdt>Adoption and feedback \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Usage, satisfaction and time saved.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Counting mandated use as success.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-request-queue-that-ate-the-roadmap\">Incident: The request queue that ate the roadmap\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A platform team answered every request in order, and after a quarter had shipped nothing that reduced requests.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"platformctl requests --last 90d --by type\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>platformctl requests --last 90d --by \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">type\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">database-request        214\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pipeline-setup          167\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">access-request           98\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">namespace-request        76\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The team had treated itself as a support desk. Looking at the counts, they saw that two request types made up most of the work and both were repeatable. They paused new features for a month, automated the database and pipeline requests as self-service workflows, and watched the queue fall by more than half the next quarter. The measure of success became requests that no longer needed to exist.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Prioritising by who asks loudest\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building the whole platform before anyone uses a piece of it\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Counting mandated usage as proof of value\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Publishing no roadmap or release notes\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never closing the loop with people who gave feedback\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-with-users-not-tools","text":"Start with users, not tools"},{"id":"rank-by-pain-and-reach","text":"Rank by pain and reach"},{"id":"the-thinnest-viable-platform","text":"The thinnest viable platform"},{"id":"make-adoption-a-choice","text":"Make adoption a choice"},{"id":"a-roadmap-people-can-read","text":"A roadmap people can read"},{"id":"feedback-loops","text":"Feedback loops"},{"id":"name-an-owner-and-a-customer-for-each-capability","text":"Name an owner and a customer for each capability"},{"id":"incident-the-request-queue-that-ate-the-roadmap","text":"Incident: The request queue that ate the roadmap"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"CNCF platforms white paper","u":"https://tag-app-delivery.cncf.io/whitepapers/platforms/"},{"t":"Team Topologies key concepts","u":"https://teamtopologies.com/key-concepts"}],"text":"The most common reason a platform fails is not technical. It is that nobody treated it as something people have to want. Internal users usually have a choice, even when it is unofficial: they can keep their own scripts, buy a managed service or ignore the platform and ask a friendly engineer for help. A platform that wins has to be better than those alternatives. What you will learn How to identify users and their jobs How to find the pain worth fixing first The idea of the thinnest viable platform How to run a roadmap, feedback and release notes Start with users, not tools List the groups who use or are affected by the platform: product developers, data engineers, security reviewers, new joiners, on-call engineers. For each, write the jobs they are trying to get done: \"deploy a change to production\", \"get a database for a prototype\", \"see why my service is slow\". Interview a handful of them and watch them work. You will find friction that no survey would reveal, such as a copied command that everyone has to edit by hand. A useful habit is to record each interview as a short journey: step, time taken, who helped, what went wrong. Add the steps up. The slowest and most painful steps across many teams are candidates for the first release. Rank by pain and reach For each problem, estimate how many teams have it, how often, and how much time it costs. Multiply roughly and sort. This avoids two common errors: fixing the loudest complaint, and fixing the most interesting problem. The ranking also gives you a defensible answer when someone asks why their request is not first. problems = [ ( \"get a database\" , 11 , 6 , 4.0 ), ( \"add a pipeline\" , 12 , 3 , 6.0 ), ( \"rotate a secret\" , 8 , 12 , 0.5 ), ( \"create a dashboard\" , 9 , 2 , 2.0 ), ] # teams affected, times per year, hours each time ranked = sorted (problems, key= lambda p: p[ 1 ] * p[ 2 ] * p[ 3 ], reverse= True ) for name, teams, times, hours in ranked: print ( f\" {name: 20 } {teams * times * hours: 6.0 f} hours per year\" ) The thinnest viable platform Team Topologies uses the phrase \"thinnest viable platform\" for the smallest set of capabilities that lets product teams move faster. It might be a documented wiki page at first, then a template, then a self-service workflow. Each step earns the next. Avoid the temptation to build the full architecture diagram before anyone has used a single piece. Make adoption a choice Mandates give you usage but hide quality. If you can, let teams opt in, and watch what they choose. A rising share of new services created through the platform, and teams coming back for the second and third thing, are better signals than a mandate. Where a mandate is justified, for example a security control, make it narrow and explain why. A roadmap people can read Publish a short roadmap with three horizons: now, next and later. State the problem each item solves and the users it helps, not only the technology. Say what you are not doing and why. Keep a visible changelog with release notes written for users: what changed, what they need to do and what they gain. Feedback loops Collect feedback in more than one way: an office hour, a channel with an on-call rotation for questions, a short survey after common tasks, and direct interviews each quarter. Close the loop. When someone reports a problem, tell them what happened. A platform team that never answers will soon stop being told. Name an owner and a customer for each capability Each capability needs a product owner on the platform side and, ideally, a named pilot customer on the product side. The pilot customer tells you whether the capability solves a real job, and becomes a reference for others. The platform product loop Flow in order: 1, Users and jobs to Ranked pain; 2, Ranked pain to Thinnest slice; 3, Thinnest slice to Release and notes; 4, Release and notes to Adoption and feedback; 5, Adoption and feedback to Users and jobs (next round). Outside party Observability Step Service Numbered arrows show "},"platform/golden-paths-and-paved-roads":{"html":"\u003cp>Every team wants to move fast and nobody wants to rediscover how to set up logging. A golden path, sometimes called a paved road, is the platform&#39;s answer: a documented, automated, opinionated way to go from an idea to a running, observable, secure service. It is a path, not a wall. People can step off it when they have a good reason, and they know what they give up when they do.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a golden path contains\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to choose which paths to build\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to design for the common case and still allow exits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to keep a path alive as technology changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-a-golden-path-is-made-of\">What a golden path is made of\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A good path covers the whole journey for one kind of workload, for example &quot;a stateless HTTP service in Go&quot; or &quot;a scheduled data job in Python&quot;. Typically it includes:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A repository template with the right layout and tooling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A pipeline with build, test, scan and deploy steps\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A runtime definition with sensible resources, probes and scaling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Default logging, metrics, tracing and an initial dashboard\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Security basics such as an image policy and secret handling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Documentation and an owner field in the catalog\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Each piece is something the team would otherwise assemble by hand. Together they mean the first deploy takes an afternoon, not weeks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"choose-paths-by-demand\">Choose paths by demand\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Look at what teams build. If 60 percent of new services are stateless web APIs, build that path first. Do not build a path for a one-off workload. A short list of paths that cover most cases beats a long list that is hard to maintain. A reasonable starting set is: web service, background worker, scheduled job and static or frontend site.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"opinionated-with-exits\">Opinionated, with exits\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A path works because it makes decisions for the user: which language versions, which base image, which pipeline stages, which dashboards. Too many options recreate the problem. But a path with no exit forces teams with unusual needs to fight it or leave. The usual pattern is to make the path the default and supported option, and offer a documented way to customise individual layers, for example adding an extra sidecar or a different build step, while keeping the guardrails that matter: ownership metadata, scanning and observability.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>State what support looks like on and off the path. On the path, the platform team handles upgrades and answers questions. Off the path, the team owns more, and the platform may only support the common pieces.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"make-the-right-thing-the-easy-thing\">Make the right thing the easy thing\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Do not rely on rules and review to push people onto the path. Rely on convenience. If the path creates a repository, wires the pipeline and shows a green deploy in ten minutes, people will use it. If it needs three tickets, they will not.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-what-a-service-has\">Check what a service has\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You can measure how much of a service follows the path by checking its metadata. The snippet below counts which required pieces a service declares.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"required = {&quot;owner&quot;, &quot;pipeline&quot;, &quot;dashboard&quot;, &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;slo&quot;}\n\nservices = {\n    &quot;checkout&quot;: {&quot;owner&quot;, &quot;pipeline&quot;, &quot;dashboard&quot;, &quot;runbook&quot;, &quot;slo&quot;},\n    &quot;search&quot;: {&quot;owner&quot;, &quot;pipeline&quot;, &quot;dashboard&quot;},\n    &quot;legacy-report&quot;: {&quot;pipeline&quot;},\n}\n\nfor name, have in services.items():\n    missing = sorted(required - have)\n    status = &quot;on path&quot; if not missing else &quot;missing &quot; + &quot;, &quot;.join(missing)\n    print(f&quot;{name:14} {status}&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">required = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pipeline&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dashboard&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;runbook&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;slo&quot;\u003c/span>}\n\nservices = {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;checkout&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pipeline&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dashboard&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;runbook&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;slo&quot;\u003c/span>},\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;search&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pipeline&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dashboard&quot;\u003c/span>},\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;legacy-report&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pipeline&quot;\u003c/span>},\n}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> name, have \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> services.items():\n    missing = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sorted\u003c/span>(required - have)\n    status = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;on path&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> missing \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;missing &quot;\u003c/span> + \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;, &quot;\u003c/span>.join(missing)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{name:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">14\u003c/span>}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{status}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-the-path-alive\">Keep the path alive\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A path is a product with a lifecycle. Version it. When a language version, base image or library has a security fix, update the template and give existing services an automatic pull request, so they stay current without manual work. Deprecate old versions with a date and a migration guide. Track how many services use each version, because a path that nobody updates becomes a trap.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"avoid-the-two-failure-modes\">Avoid the two failure modes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The first is the abandoned path: built with fanfare, never updated, now a source of outdated defaults. The second is the cage: a path so rigid that every real need requires an exception. Review exceptions regularly. A repeated exception is a signal to improve the path.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"tmpl&gt;ci;ci&gt;run;run&gt;obs;run&gt;sec;obs&gt;own;sec&gt;own\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A golden path for a web service\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tmpl\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Template\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ci\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"run\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Runtime\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"obs\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sec\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Security\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"own\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Catalog entry\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Template to Pipeline; 2, Pipeline to Runtime; 3, Runtime to Observability; 4, Runtime to Security; 5, Observability to Catalog entry; 6, Security to Catalog entry.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tmpl\">\u003cdt>Template \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Repository with layout, tests and tooling.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Outdated language or base image.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ci\">\u003cdt>Pipeline \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Build, test, scan and deploy.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Steps copied and edited by hand.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"run\">\u003cdt>Runtime \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Defaults for resources, probes and scaling.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Defaults that do not fit the workload.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"obs\">\u003cdt>Observability \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Logs, metrics, traces and a dashboard.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A dashboard nobody looks at.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sec\">\u003cdt>Security \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Image policy and secret handling.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Checks that can be skipped silently.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"own\">\u003cdt>Catalog entry \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Owner, tier and runbook link.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Metadata that is never filled in.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-cage-and-the-escape\">Incident: The cage and the escape\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A golden path allowed only one language version. A team needing a newer runtime for a security fix could not get it, so it built its own pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"platformctl path status web-service\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>platformctl path status web-service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">path version:    3.2\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">runtime pinned:  17\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">services on 3.2: 41\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">services off path: 9 (7 cite &quot;runtime version&quot;)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The platform team saw that nine services had left the path and seven gave the same reason. They added a supported choice of two runtime versions, with an automatic upgrade pull request for the older one. Within two months most of the nine had come back, because staying on the path was now easier than maintaining their own pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Building a path for a workload only one team has\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving the path no exit, so teams leave it entirely\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting to update templates after launch\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Counting services that used the template once as &quot;on the path&quot; years later\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring paths by how many rules they enforce instead of how fast they get people to production\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-a-golden-path-is-made-of","text":"What a golden path is made of"},{"id":"choose-paths-by-demand","text":"Choose paths by demand"},{"id":"opinionated-with-exits","text":"Opinionated, with exits"},{"id":"make-the-right-thing-the-easy-thing","text":"Make the right thing the easy thing"},{"id":"check-what-a-service-has","text":"Check what a service has"},{"id":"keep-the-path-alive","text":"Keep the path alive"},{"id":"avoid-the-two-failure-modes","text":"Avoid the two failure modes"},{"id":"incident-the-cage-and-the-escape","text":"Incident: The cage and the escape"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Backstage: software templates","u":"https://backstage.io/docs/features/software-templates/"},{"t":"CNCF platforms white paper","u":"https://tag-app-delivery.cncf.io/whitepapers/platforms/"}],"text":"Every team wants to move fast and nobody wants to rediscover how to set up logging. A golden path, sometimes called a paved road, is the platform&#39;s answer: a documented, automated, opinionated way to go from an idea to a running, observable, secure service. It is a path, not a wall. People can step off it when they have a good reason, and they know what they give up when they do. What you will learn What a golden path contains How to choose which paths to build How to design for the common case and still allow exits How to keep a path alive as technology changes What a golden path is made of A good path covers the whole journey for one kind of workload, for example \"a stateless HTTP service in Go\" or \"a scheduled data job in Python\". Typically it includes: A repository template with the right layout and tooling A pipeline with build, test, scan and deploy steps A runtime definition with sensible resources, probes and scaling Default logging, metrics, tracing and an initial dashboard Security basics such as an image policy and secret handling Documentation and an owner field in the catalog Each piece is something the team would otherwise assemble by hand. Together they mean the first deploy takes an afternoon, not weeks. Choose paths by demand Look at what teams build. If 60 percent of new services are stateless web APIs, build that path first. Do not build a path for a one-off workload. A short list of paths that cover most cases beats a long list that is hard to maintain. A reasonable starting set is: web service, background worker, scheduled job and static or frontend site. Opinionated, with exits A path works because it makes decisions for the user: which language versions, which base image, which pipeline stages, which dashboards. Too many options recreate the problem. But a path with no exit forces teams with unusual needs to fight it or leave. The usual pattern is to make the path the default and supported option, and offer a documented way to customise individual layers, for example adding an extra sidecar or a different build step, while keeping the guardrails that matter: ownership metadata, scanning and observability. State what support looks like on and off the path. On the path, the platform team handles upgrades and answers questions. Off the path, the team owns more, and the platform may only support the common pieces. Make the right thing the easy thing Do not rely on rules and review to push people onto the path. Rely on convenience. If the path creates a repository, wires the pipeline and shows a green deploy in ten minutes, people will use it. If it needs three tickets, they will not. Check what a service has You can measure how much of a service follows the path by checking its metadata. The snippet below counts which required pieces a service declares. required = { \"owner\" , \"pipeline\" , \"dashboard\" , \"runbook\" , \"slo\" } services = { \"checkout\" : { \"owner\" , \"pipeline\" , \"dashboard\" , \"runbook\" , \"slo\" }, \"search\" : { \"owner\" , \"pipeline\" , \"dashboard\" }, \"legacy-report\" : { \"pipeline\" }, } for name, have in services.items(): missing = sorted (required - have) status = \"on path\" if not missing else \"missing \" + \", \" .join(missing) print ( f\" {name: 14 } {status} \" ) Keep the path alive A path is a product with a lifecycle. Version it. When a language version, base image or library has a security fix, update the template and give existing services an automatic pull request, so they stay current without manual work. Deprecate old versions with a date and a migration guide. Track how many services use each version, because a path that nobody updates becomes a trap. Avoid the two failure modes The first is the abandoned path: built with fanfare, never updated, now a source of outdated defaults. The second is the cage: a path so rigid that every real need requires an exception. Review exceptions regularly. A repeated exception is a signal to improve the path. A golden path for a web service Flow in order: 1,"},"platform/anatomy-of-an-internal-developer-platform":{"html":"\u003cp>An internal developer platform, or IDP, is not one product. It is a set of layers that together let a developer ask for something and get a safe, working result. Understanding the layers helps you decide what to build, what to buy and where the seams will hurt.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The main planes of a platform and what each does\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How a single request flows through them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Where open source tools commonly fit\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to avoid rebuilding everything at once\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-planes\">The planes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Reference architectures from the CNCF and from vendors describe slightly different layers, but the idea is stable. A useful five-part view is:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Plane\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Purpose\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Examples of what lives here\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Developer control\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Where people ask for things\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Portal, command line, templates, catalog\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Integration and delivery\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Turns code into running software\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Source control, pipelines, registries, deployment tools\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Resource orchestration\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Creates the infrastructure behind requests\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Infrastructure as code, Crossplane, operators, cluster provisioning\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Monitoring and logging\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Shows what is happening\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Metrics, logs, traces, dashboards, alerts\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Security\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Controls identity, secrets and policy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Identity provider, secret store, policy engine, scanners\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The labels vary, but a request always touches all of them: a developer asks for a service, a pipeline builds it, infrastructure appears, monitoring is wired up and security checks run.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"follow-one-request\">Follow one request\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Suppose a developer wants a new web service with a database.\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>They pick a template in the portal and fill in a name and owner.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The platform creates a repository and registers the service in the catalog.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The pipeline builds the first version, scans it and deploys to a development environment.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A resource request creates a database through the orchestration layer, with credentials stored in the secret store.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Default dashboards and alerts appear because the template declared them.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Policy checks confirm the service has an owner, resource limits and an approved image.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>If any step needs a ticket, the platform has a gap. Mapping a request like this is a good way to find the gaps.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-catalog-at-the-centre\">A catalog at the centre\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Most successful platforms keep a catalog: a record of every service, its owner, its dependencies and its tier. The catalog is what other planes read. The pipeline reads the owner to route failures. Monitoring reads the tier to set alert severity. Policy reads the data class to decide what is allowed. Without it, each tool keeps its own incomplete picture.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"compose-do-not-monolith\">Compose, do not monolith\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>You do not need to build a platform in one go, and you rarely need to build every layer yourself. Most platforms are composed of existing tools with a thin layer of glue and a good front door. Decide, for each capability, whether to build, buy or adopt open source, and write down why. The seams between tools are where reliability and user experience break, so spend your effort there.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"standards-at-the-seams\">Standards at the seams\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use open standards where they exist so that parts can be replaced: container images and registries follow OCI specifications, telemetry can use OpenTelemetry, deployments can be declared as Kubernetes resources, and identity can use OpenID Connect. Each standard lowers the cost of changing one part later.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"model-the-platform-s-contract\">Model the platform&#39;s contract\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Describe each capability as a contract: what the user asks for, what they get, what the service level is and who to contact. The snippet below checks that a list of capabilities has the fields a contract needs.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"capabilities = [\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;new-service&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;template, name, owner&quot;, &quot;output&quot;: &quot;repo, pipeline, dev env&quot;, &quot;slo&quot;: &quot;ready in 15 minutes&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;platform-delivery&quot;},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;database&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;engine, size, env&quot;, &quot;output&quot;: &quot;instance and secret&quot;, &quot;slo&quot;: &quot;ready in 10 minutes&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;platform-data&quot;},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;dashboard&quot;, &quot;input&quot;: &quot;service&quot;, &quot;output&quot;: &quot;default dashboard&quot;},\n]\n\nneed = {&quot;name&quot;, &quot;input&quot;, &quot;output&quot;, &quot;slo&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;}\nfor c in capabilities:\n    gap = sorted(need - set(c))\n    print(c[&quot;name&quot;], &quot;complete&quot; if not gap else &quot;missing &quot; + &quot;, &quot;.join(gap))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">capabilities = [\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;new-service&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;input&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;template, name, owner&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;output&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;repo, pipeline, dev env&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;slo&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ready in 15 minutes&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;platform-delivery&quot;\u003c/span>},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;database&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;input&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;engine, size, env&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;output&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;instance and secret&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;slo&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ready in 10 minutes&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;platform-data&quot;\u003c/span>},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dashboard&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;input&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;service&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;output&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;default dashboard&quot;\u003c/span>},\n]\n\nneed = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;input&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;output&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;slo&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> capabilities:\n    gap = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sorted\u003c/span>(need - \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span>(c))\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(c[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;complete&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> gap \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;missing &quot;\u003c/span> + \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;, &quot;\u003c/span>.join(gap))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"4\" data-edges=\"ctl&gt;cat;ctl&gt;del;del&gt;res;res&gt;mon;cat&gt;sec;sec&gt;del\" style=\"--rows:4\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">One request across the planes\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"12.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"87.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"87.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"12.5\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"87.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"87.5\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ctl\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Developer control\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cat\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:12.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Catalog\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"del\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Delivery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"res\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Resource orchestration\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"mon\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:87.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Monitoring\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sec\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:87.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Security and policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Developer control to Catalog; 2, Developer control to Delivery; 3, Delivery to Resource orchestration; 4, Resource orchestration to Monitoring; 5, Catalog to Security and policy; 6, Security and policy to Delivery.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ctl\">\u003cdt>Developer control \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Portal, command line and templates.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A form that does not match what the system accepts.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cat\">\u003cdt>Catalog \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Owners, tiers and dependencies.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Stale or incomplete records.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"del\">\u003cdt>Delivery \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pipeline, registry and deployment.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Skipped checks.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"res\">\u003cdt>Resource orchestration \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Infrastructure created on request.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Resources with no owner.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"mon\">\u003cdt>Monitoring \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Dashboards and alerts from defaults.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Alerts with no routing.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sec\">\u003cdt>Security and policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Identity, secrets and rules.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rules applied in one plane only.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-ticket-hidden-in-the-flow\">Incident: The ticket hidden in the flow\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A new platform advertised &quot;service in 15 minutes&quot;, yet teams reported waiting days. Nobody could say where the time went.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"platformctl trace new-service req-5530\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>platformctl trace new-service req-5530\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">portal-submit       00:00\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">repo-created        00:02\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">pipeline-first-run  00:09\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">database-requested  00:12\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">database-approved   2d 04:10   (manual approval)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Tracing one request showed that the database step waited for a manual approval by a person in another team. The platform team agreed a size limit and an automatic approval for small development databases, kept human review for production, and the end-to-end time for the common case dropped to under twenty minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Building every plane before any user has tried the first one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Keeping no catalog, so each tool has its own partial picture\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring hand-offs and manual approvals in the middle of a flow\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Locking into a tool with no standard interface\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Describing capabilities by technology instead of by what users get\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-planes","text":"The planes"},{"id":"follow-one-request","text":"Follow one request"},{"id":"a-catalog-at-the-centre","text":"A catalog at the centre"},{"id":"compose-do-not-monolith","text":"Compose, do not monolith"},{"id":"standards-at-the-seams","text":"Standards at the seams"},{"id":"model-the-platform-s-contract","text":"Model the platform's contract"},{"id":"incident-the-ticket-hidden-in-the-flow","text":"Incident: The ticket hidden in the flow"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"CNCF platforms white paper","u":"https://tag-app-delivery.cncf.io/whitepapers/platforms/"},{"t":"Open Container Initiative","u":"https://opencontainers.org/"}],"text":"An internal developer platform, or IDP, is not one product. It is a set of layers that together let a developer ask for something and get a safe, working result. Understanding the layers helps you decide what to build, what to buy and where the seams will hurt. What you will learn The main planes of a platform and what each does How a single request flows through them Where open source tools commonly fit How to avoid rebuilding everything at once The planes Reference architectures from the CNCF and from vendors describe slightly different layers, but the idea is stable. A useful five-part view is: Plane Purpose Examples of what lives here Developer control Where people ask for things Portal, command line, templates, catalog Integration and delivery Turns code into running software Source control, pipelines, registries, deployment tools Resource orchestration Creates the infrastructure behind requests Infrastructure as code, Crossplane, operators, cluster provisioning Monitoring and logging Shows what is happening Metrics, logs, traces, dashboards, alerts Security Controls identity, secrets and policy Identity provider, secret store, policy engine, scanners The labels vary, but a request always touches all of them: a developer asks for a service, a pipeline builds it, infrastructure appears, monitoring is wired up and security checks run. Follow one request Suppose a developer wants a new web service with a database. They pick a template in the portal and fill in a name and owner. The platform creates a repository and registers the service in the catalog. The pipeline builds the first version, scans it and deploys to a development environment. A resource request creates a database through the orchestration layer, with credentials stored in the secret store. Default dashboards and alerts appear because the template declared them. Policy checks confirm the service has an owner, resource limits and an approved image. If any step needs a ticket, the platform has a gap. Mapping a request like this is a good way to find the gaps. A catalog at the centre Most successful platforms keep a catalog: a record of every service, its owner, its dependencies and its tier. The catalog is what other planes read. The pipeline reads the owner to route failures. Monitoring reads the tier to set alert severity. Policy reads the data class to decide what is allowed. Without it, each tool keeps its own incomplete picture. Compose, do not monolith You do not need to build a platform in one go, and you rarely need to build every layer yourself. Most platforms are composed of existing tools with a thin layer of glue and a good front door. Decide, for each capability, whether to build, buy or adopt open source, and write down why. The seams between tools are where reliability and user experience break, so spend your effort there. Standards at the seams Use open standards where they exist so that parts can be replaced: container images and registries follow OCI specifications, telemetry can use OpenTelemetry, deployments can be declared as Kubernetes resources, and identity can use OpenID Connect. Each standard lowers the cost of changing one part later. Model the platform&#39;s contract Describe each capability as a contract: what the user asks for, what they get, what the service level is and who to contact. The snippet below checks that a list of capabilities has the fields a contract needs. capabilities = [ { \"name\" : \"new-service\" , \"input\" : \"template, name, owner\" , \"output\" : \"repo, pipeline, dev env\" , \"slo\" : \"ready in 15 minutes\" , \"owner\" : \"platform-delivery\" }, { \"name\" : \"database\" , \"input\" : \"engine, size, env\" , \"output\" : \"instance and secret\" , \"slo\" : \"ready in 10 minutes\" , \"owner\" : \"platform-data\" }, { \"name\" : \"dashboard\" , \"input\" : \"service\" , \"output\" : \"default dashboard\" }, ] need = { \"name\" , \"input\" , \"output\" , \"slo\" , \"owner\" } for c in capabilities: gap = sorted (need - set (c)) print (c[ \"name\" ], \"complete\" if not gap e"},"platform/developer-portals-and-the-service-catalog":{"html":"\u003cp>Ask a large engineering organisation &quot;who owns this service?&quot; and the honest answer is often &quot;let me ask around&quot;. A service catalog fixes that by keeping a living record of services, their owners and their links. A developer portal puts the catalog, the templates, the docs and the status of your services in one place. Together they are the visible part of a platform.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a catalog holds and why accuracy matters more than size\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How a portal such as Backstage is organised\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How catalog entries are defined and discovered\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to keep entries from going stale\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-belongs-in-a-catalog\">What belongs in a catalog\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The minimum useful entry answers four questions about a service: who owns it, what is it, how do I run or reach it, and what does it depend on. Practical fields are:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Name, description and lifecycle (experimental, production, deprecated)\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Owning team and an on-call contact\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tier or criticality, and the data classes it handles\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Links to the repository, pipeline, dashboard, runbook and documentation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Dependencies on other services, databases and queues\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Other kinds of things can live in the same catalog: APIs, libraries, infrastructure resources, teams and people. The relationships between them are what make it more than a spreadsheet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"how-backstage-models-it\">How Backstage models it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Backstage is an open source developer portal, started at Spotify and now a CNCF project. Its software catalog is built from descriptor files, typically named \u003ccode>catalog-info.yaml\u003c/code>, stored next to the code. Each describes an entity with a kind, such as Component, API, Resource, System, Domain, Group or User.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1\nkind: Component\nmetadata:\n  name: checkout\n  description: Handles cart and payment orchestration\n  annotations:\n    github.com/project-slug: example-org/checkout\nspec:\n  type: service\n  lifecycle: production\n  owner: team-payments\n  system: shop\n  dependsOn:\n    - resource:default/orders-db\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">backstage.io/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Component\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">checkout\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">description:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Handles\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">cart\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">and\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">payment\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orchestration\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">annotations:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">github.com/project-slug:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">example-org/checkout\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">service\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">lifecycle:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">production\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">owner:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">team-payments\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">system:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">dependsOn:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">resource:default/orders-db\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Because the descriptor lives with the code, the owner of the service updates it in the same pull request that changes the service. The portal discovers files from source control and keeps the catalog in sync. This is a more reliable approach than a central team editing a spreadsheet.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"more-than-a-catalog\">More than a catalog\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A portal usually adds more surfaces on top of the catalog. Software templates create new services from a form. Documentation systems such as TechDocs render Markdown from the repository. Plugins show pipeline status, deployments, alerts, cost and security findings against each service. The value is in seeing everything about a service on one page without opening six tools.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"accuracy-beats-volume\">Accuracy beats volume\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A catalog with ten thousand entries of unknown quality is worse than one with a hundred accurate entries. Wrong owners send people to the wrong team at 3am. Design for accuracy:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Make ownership required, and reject entries without a valid team\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Store descriptors with the code and review them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Check links and owners automatically and report broken ones\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Mark entries not updated for a long time and ask the owner to confirm or retire them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remove or archive services that no longer exist\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"run-a-freshness-check\">Run a freshness check\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The snippet below flags catalog entries whose owner does not exist or that have not been confirmed recently.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"teams = {&quot;team-payments&quot;, &quot;team-search&quot;, &quot;team-platform&quot;}\ntoday = 400  # day number, for the example\nentries = [\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;checkout&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;team-payments&quot;, &quot;confirmed&quot;: 390},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;legacy-report&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;team-old&quot;, &quot;confirmed&quot;: 120},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;search-api&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;team-search&quot;, &quot;confirmed&quot;: 250},\n]\n\nfor e in entries:\n    problems = []\n    if e[&quot;owner&quot;] not in teams:\n        problems.append(&quot;owner not found&quot;)\n    if today - e[&quot;confirmed&quot;] &gt; 180:\n        problems.append(&quot;not confirmed in 180 days&quot;)\n    print(f&quot;{e['name']:14}&quot;, &quot;, &quot;.join(problems) or &quot;ok&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">teams = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team-payments&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team-search&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team-platform&quot;\u003c/span>}\ntoday = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">400\u003c/span>  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># day number, for the example\u003c/span>\nentries = [\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;checkout&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team-payments&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;confirmed&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">390\u003c/span>},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;legacy-report&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team-old&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;confirmed&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">120\u003c/span>},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;search-api&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team-search&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;confirmed&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">250\u003c/span>},\n]\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> e \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> entries:\n    problems = []\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> teams:\n        problems.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner not found&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> today - e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;confirmed&quot;\u003c/span>] &gt; \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">180\u003c/span>:\n        problems.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;not confirmed in 180 days&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;\u003c/span>]:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">14\u003c/span>}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;, &quot;\u003c/span>.join(problems) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">or\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ok&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"make-it-useful-before-it-is-complete\">Make it useful before it is complete\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Start with the services people ask about most, such as the critical tier. Show something people want in the portal early, for example a single page that links a service to its dashboard and runbook. When the catalog saves someone time during an incident, the owners will keep it accurate themselves.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"do-not-build-what-you-can-adopt\">Do not build what you can adopt\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Building a portal from scratch is a large, permanent commitment. Backstage is a framework, so adopting it still takes engineering work to deploy, extend and maintain. Compare it with commercial portals and with a simpler option, such as a generated documentation site backed by a catalog file. Choose the smallest tool that gives you the catalog and the front door.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"2\" data-edges=\"repo&gt;disc;disc&gt;cat;cat&gt;portal;portal&gt;user\" style=\"--rows:2\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From code to portal\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"75.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"75.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"repo\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Service repository\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"disc\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Catalog discovery\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cat\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Software catalog\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"portal\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Developer portal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"user\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:75.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Developers and on-call\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Service repository to Catalog discovery; 2, Catalog discovery to Software catalog; 3, Software catalog to Developer portal; 4, Developer portal to Developers and on-call.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"repo\">\u003cdt>Service repository \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Holds code and its catalog descriptor.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Descriptor missing or outdated.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"disc\">\u003cdt>Catalog discovery \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Reads descriptors from source control.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rate limits or stale sync.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cat\">\u003cdt>Software catalog \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Entities, owners and relations.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Entries with no valid owner.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"portal\">\u003cdt>Developer portal \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Search, pages, templates and docs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too many plugins, slow pages.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"user\">\u003cdt>Developers and on-call \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Find owners, runbooks and status.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Data they cannot trust.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-page-that-went-to-the-wrong-team\">Incident: The page that went to the wrong team\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>At 02:10 an alert on the reporting service paged a team that had not owned it for a year, and nobody realised for forty minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"platformctl catalog show reporting-api\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>platformctl catalog show reporting-api\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">owner:      team-old   (team not found in directory)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">lifecycle:  production\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">confirmed:  2025-02-03\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The service had moved teams during a reorganisation, but the descriptor kept the old owner and nothing checked it against the team directory. After the incident the platform team added a nightly check that failed any entry whose owner is not a real team, and a monthly report of entries not confirmed in six months.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating a catalog as a one-off data import\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting a central team edit entries instead of the service owners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Allowing entries with no owner\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Installing many portal plugins before the catalog is trustworthy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Building a portal from scratch when an existing one would do\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"what-belongs-in-a-catalog","text":"What belongs in a catalog"},{"id":"how-backstage-models-it","text":"How Backstage models it"},{"id":"more-than-a-catalog","text":"More than a catalog"},{"id":"accuracy-beats-volume","text":"Accuracy beats volume"},{"id":"run-a-freshness-check","text":"Run a freshness check"},{"id":"make-it-useful-before-it-is-complete","text":"Make it useful before it is complete"},{"id":"do-not-build-what-you-can-adopt","text":"Do not build what you can adopt"},{"id":"incident-the-page-that-went-to-the-wrong-team","text":"Incident: The page that went to the wrong team"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Backstage: software catalog","u":"https://backstage.io/docs/features/software-catalog/"},{"t":"Backstage: overview","u":"https://backstage.io/docs/overview/what-is-backstage"}],"text":"Ask a large engineering organisation \"who owns this service?\" and the honest answer is often \"let me ask around\". A service catalog fixes that by keeping a living record of services, their owners and their links. A developer portal puts the catalog, the templates, the docs and the status of your services in one place. Together they are the visible part of a platform. What you will learn What a catalog holds and why accuracy matters more than size How a portal such as Backstage is organised How catalog entries are defined and discovered How to keep entries from going stale What belongs in a catalog The minimum useful entry answers four questions about a service: who owns it, what is it, how do I run or reach it, and what does it depend on. Practical fields are: Name, description and lifecycle (experimental, production, deprecated) Owning team and an on-call contact Tier or criticality, and the data classes it handles Links to the repository, pipeline, dashboard, runbook and documentation Dependencies on other services, databases and queues Other kinds of things can live in the same catalog: APIs, libraries, infrastructure resources, teams and people. The relationships between them are what make it more than a spreadsheet. How Backstage models it Backstage is an open source developer portal, started at Spotify and now a CNCF project. Its software catalog is built from descriptor files, typically named catalog-info.yaml , stored next to the code. Each describes an entity with a kind, such as Component, API, Resource, System, Domain, Group or User. apiVersion: backstage.io/v1alpha1 kind: Component metadata: name: checkout description: Handles cart and payment orchestration annotations: github.com/project-slug: example-org/checkout spec: type: service lifecycle: production owner: team-payments system: shop dependsOn: - resource:default/orders-db Because the descriptor lives with the code, the owner of the service updates it in the same pull request that changes the service. The portal discovers files from source control and keeps the catalog in sync. This is a more reliable approach than a central team editing a spreadsheet. More than a catalog A portal usually adds more surfaces on top of the catalog. Software templates create new services from a form. Documentation systems such as TechDocs render Markdown from the repository. Plugins show pipeline status, deployments, alerts, cost and security findings against each service. The value is in seeing everything about a service on one page without opening six tools. Accuracy beats volume A catalog with ten thousand entries of unknown quality is worse than one with a hundred accurate entries. Wrong owners send people to the wrong team at 3am. Design for accuracy: Make ownership required, and reject entries without a valid team Store descriptors with the code and review them Check links and owners automatically and report broken ones Mark entries not updated for a long time and ask the owner to confirm or retire them Remove or archive services that no longer exist Run a freshness check The snippet below flags catalog entries whose owner does not exist or that have not been confirmed recently. teams = { \"team-payments\" , \"team-search\" , \"team-platform\" } today = 400 # day number, for the example entries = [ { \"name\" : \"checkout\" , \"owner\" : \"team-payments\" , \"confirmed\" : 390 }, { \"name\" : \"legacy-report\" , \"owner\" : \"team-old\" , \"confirmed\" : 120 }, { \"name\" : \"search-api\" , \"owner\" : \"team-search\" , \"confirmed\" : 250 }, ] for e in entries: problems = [] if e[ \"owner\" ] not in teams: problems.append( \"owner not found\" ) if today - e[ \"confirmed\" ] > 180 : problems.append( \"not confirmed in 180 days\" ) print ( f\" {e[ &#x27;name&#x27; ]: 14 } \" , \", \" .join(problems) or \"ok\" ) Make it useful before it is complete Start with the services people ask about most, such as the critical tier. Show something people want in the portal early, for example a single page that links a service to its da"},"platform/scaffolding-templates-and-self-service-workflows":{"html":"\u003cp>A path on paper is not much help. People follow the path that is one click away. Scaffolding is the mechanism that turns a request such as &quot;new Go service called billing, owned by team payments&quot; into a repository, a working pipeline, a catalog entry and a first deployment, without anyone filing a ticket.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>What a good template asks for and generates\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to validate input and apply policy before creating anything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How self-service differs from &quot;self-serve with approval&quot;\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to keep generated repositories updatable\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ask-few-questions\">Ask few questions\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A template form should ask only what it cannot infer: a name, an owning team, a short description, maybe the language and a tier. Fewer fields mean faster use and fewer mistakes. Derive everything else: repository name from service name, namespace from team, alert routing from owner. Offer safe defaults and hide advanced options behind an extra step.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validate-before-you-create\">Validate before you create\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Check input early and give clear errors. Names should follow the naming convention and be unique. The team must exist. The tier must be one of the allowed values. A template that fails halfway, leaving a half-created repository, is worse than one that refuses at the start.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import re\n\nTEAMS = {&quot;payments&quot;, &quot;search&quot;, &quot;platform&quot;}\nTIERS = {&quot;critical&quot;, &quot;standard&quot;, &quot;experimental&quot;}\n\ndef validate(req):\n    errors = []\n    if not re.fullmatch(r&quot;[a-z][a-z0-9-]{2,29}&quot;, req.get(&quot;name&quot;, &quot;&quot;)):\n        errors.append(&quot;name must be 3-30 lowercase letters, digits or hyphens, starting with a letter&quot;)\n    if req.get(&quot;team&quot;) not in TEAMS:\n        errors.append(&quot;team is not known&quot;)\n    if req.get(&quot;tier&quot;) not in TIERS:\n        errors.append(&quot;tier must be one of &quot; + &quot;, &quot;.join(sorted(TIERS)))\n    return errors\n\nprint(validate({&quot;name&quot;: &quot;billing&quot;, &quot;team&quot;: &quot;payments&quot;, &quot;tier&quot;: &quot;standard&quot;}))\nprint(validate({&quot;name&quot;: &quot;Billing!&quot;, &quot;team&quot;: &quot;ghost&quot;, &quot;tier&quot;: &quot;gold&quot;}))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> re\n\nTEAMS = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;payments&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;search&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;platform&quot;\u003c/span>}\nTIERS = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;critical&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;standard&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;experimental&quot;\u003c/span>}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">validate\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">req\u003c/span>):\n    errors = []\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> re.fullmatch(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">r&quot;[a-z][a-z0-9-]{2,29}&quot;\u003c/span>, req.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>)):\n        errors.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name must be 3-30 lowercase letters, digits or hyphens, starting with a letter&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> req.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> TEAMS:\n        errors.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team is not known&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> req.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> TIERS:\n        errors.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier must be one of &quot;\u003c/span> + \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;, &quot;\u003c/span>.join(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sorted\u003c/span>(TIERS)))\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> errors\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(validate({\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;billing&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;payments&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;standard&quot;\u003c/span>}))\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(validate({\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Billing!&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ghost&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;gold&quot;\u003c/span>}))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-a-template-generates\">What a template generates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A template can create several things in one flow: a repository from a skeleton, with the service name substituted; a pipeline definition; deployment manifests; a catalog descriptor; a documentation stub; monitoring dashboards and alert rules; and access for the owning team. Order the steps so that if one fails, earlier work can be undone or the flow can be resumed. Make each step idempotent, so running it twice does not create duplicates.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"self-service-and-approval\">Self-service and approval\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Self-service does not mean no control. Divide requests by risk. Low-risk, reversible requests such as a development namespace or a small database should be instant. Higher-risk requests, such as production access or large resources, can require approval, but the approval should be a step in the workflow with context shown to the approver, not a manual hand-off by email. Record who asked, who approved and what was created.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"templates-must-stay-updatable\">Templates must stay updatable\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Generating a repository once and walking away leaves hundreds of copies that drift. Plan for updates:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keep shared pipeline logic in a versioned, reusable component that repositories reference instead of copy\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Record which template version created each repository\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Offer an automatic pull request that applies template changes to existing repositories\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Test the template itself in CI by generating a sample and building it\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-the-template\">Test the template\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A template is code, so test it. Generate a service from the template in CI, build it, run its tests and deploy it to a scratch environment. A broken template breaks every new service, which makes it one of the most valuable things to test.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-the-experience-consistent\">Keep the experience consistent\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Whether a developer uses the portal form, a command line or an API, the same workflow should run. Do not build separate logic for each front end. Define the workflow once and give it several entrances.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"an-audit-trail-from-the-start\">An audit trail from the start\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Every run records its inputs, the identity of the requester, the policy decisions and the resources created. When something goes wrong, you can answer &quot;what created this, and who asked for it?&quot; in a minute.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"form&gt;check;check&gt;appr;check&gt;make;appr&gt;make;make&gt;log;make&gt;dev\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A self-service workflow\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"form\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Request form\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"check\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Validation and policy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"appr\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Approval if needed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"make\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Create resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"log\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit record\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dev\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Developer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Request form to Validation and policy; 2, Validation and policy to Approval if needed; 3, Validation and policy to Create resources; 4, Approval if needed to Create resources; 5, Create resources to Audit record; 6, Create resources to Developer.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"form\">\u003cdt>Request form \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Name, team, language and tier.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too many fields.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"check\">\u003cdt>Validation and policy \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Input checks and rules before anything is created.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Checks that run after creation.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"appr\">\u003cdt>Approval if needed \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Context-rich review for higher-risk requests.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Approval by email with no context.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"make\">\u003cdt>Create resources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Repository, pipeline, catalog entry and access.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Steps that cannot be repeated safely.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"log\">\u003cdt>Audit record \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Inputs, approver and results.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing records.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dev\">\u003cdt>Developer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Gets a working service quickly.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Waits on hidden manual steps.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-half-a-service\">Incident: Half a service\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A template failed after creating the repository but before registering the owner. For two weeks a production-bound service existed with no catalog entry and no owner.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"platformctl workflow show run-7714\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>platformctl workflow show run-7714\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">step 1 create-repo     ok\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">step 2 add-pipeline    ok\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">step 3 register-owner  error: team directory timeout\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">step 4 first-deploy    skipped\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The workflow stopped on step three and nobody was told. The platform team made each step idempotent, added automatic retry, and made failures notify the requester and the platform channel. They also added a nightly check that found any repository without a catalog entry.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Asking developers for fields the platform can derive\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Validating after creating resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating approval as an email, not a workflow step with context\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Copying pipelines into every repository instead of referencing shared ones\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Never testing the template itself\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"ask-few-questions","text":"Ask few questions"},{"id":"validate-before-you-create","text":"Validate before you create"},{"id":"what-a-template-generates","text":"What a template generates"},{"id":"self-service-and-approval","text":"Self-service and approval"},{"id":"templates-must-stay-updatable","text":"Templates must stay updatable"},{"id":"test-the-template","text":"Test the template"},{"id":"keep-the-experience-consistent","text":"Keep the experience consistent"},{"id":"an-audit-trail-from-the-start","text":"An audit trail from the start"},{"id":"incident-half-a-service","text":"Incident: Half a service"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Backstage: software templates","u":"https://backstage.io/docs/features/software-templates/"},{"t":"Argo CD documentation","u":"https://argo-cd.readthedocs.io/en/stable/"}],"text":"A path on paper is not much help. People follow the path that is one click away. Scaffolding is the mechanism that turns a request such as \"new Go service called billing, owned by team payments\" into a repository, a working pipeline, a catalog entry and a first deployment, without anyone filing a ticket. What you will learn What a good template asks for and generates How to validate input and apply policy before creating anything How self-service differs from \"self-serve with approval\" How to keep generated repositories updatable Ask few questions A template form should ask only what it cannot infer: a name, an owning team, a short description, maybe the language and a tier. Fewer fields mean faster use and fewer mistakes. Derive everything else: repository name from service name, namespace from team, alert routing from owner. Offer safe defaults and hide advanced options behind an extra step. Validate before you create Check input early and give clear errors. Names should follow the naming convention and be unique. The team must exist. The tier must be one of the allowed values. A template that fails halfway, leaving a half-created repository, is worse than one that refuses at the start. import re TEAMS = { \"payments\" , \"search\" , \"platform\" } TIERS = { \"critical\" , \"standard\" , \"experimental\" } def validate ( req ): errors = [] if not re.fullmatch( r\"[a-z][a-z0-9-]{2,29}\" , req.get( \"name\" , \"\" )): errors.append( \"name must be 3-30 lowercase letters, digits or hyphens, starting with a letter\" ) if req.get( \"team\" ) not in TEAMS: errors.append( \"team is not known\" ) if req.get( \"tier\" ) not in TIERS: errors.append( \"tier must be one of \" + \", \" .join( sorted (TIERS))) return errors print (validate({ \"name\" : \"billing\" , \"team\" : \"payments\" , \"tier\" : \"standard\" })) print (validate({ \"name\" : \"Billing!\" , \"team\" : \"ghost\" , \"tier\" : \"gold\" })) What a template generates A template can create several things in one flow: a repository from a skeleton, with the service name substituted; a pipeline definition; deployment manifests; a catalog descriptor; a documentation stub; monitoring dashboards and alert rules; and access for the owning team. Order the steps so that if one fails, earlier work can be undone or the flow can be resumed. Make each step idempotent, so running it twice does not create duplicates. Self-service and approval Self-service does not mean no control. Divide requests by risk. Low-risk, reversible requests such as a development namespace or a small database should be instant. Higher-risk requests, such as production access or large resources, can require approval, but the approval should be a step in the workflow with context shown to the approver, not a manual hand-off by email. Record who asked, who approved and what was created. Templates must stay updatable Generating a repository once and walking away leaves hundreds of copies that drift. Plan for updates: Keep shared pipeline logic in a versioned, reusable component that repositories reference instead of copy Record which template version created each repository Offer an automatic pull request that applies template changes to existing repositories Test the template itself in CI by generating a sample and building it Test the template A template is code, so test it. Generate a service from the template in CI, build it, run its tests and deploy it to a scratch environment. A broken template breaks every new service, which makes it one of the most valuable things to test. Keep the experience consistent Whether a developer uses the portal form, a command line or an API, the same workflow should run. Do not build separate logic for each front end. Define the workflow once and give it several entrances. An audit trail from the start Every run records its inputs, the identity of the requester, the policy decisions and the resources created. When something goes wrong, you can answer \"what created this, and who asked for it?\" in a minute. A self-service workflow Fl"},"platform/platform-apis-with-crossplane-and-operators":{"html":"\u003cp>A form or a pipeline creates things once. A control plane keeps them right. Kubernetes popularised the idea: you declare the state you want, and controllers continuously work to make reality match. Platform teams reuse that pattern to offer their own APIs, such as \u003ccode>Database\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>Environment\u003c/code>, and let controllers do the work behind them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Why a declarative API suits infrastructure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How Crossplane compositions map a simple request to cloud resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>When to write an operator or controller instead\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to keep the abstraction honest\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"declare-then-reconcile\">Declare, then reconcile\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>In an imperative script, you tell the system what steps to run. In a declarative API, you say what should exist, and a controller compares desired state with actual state and acts on the difference. It repeats forever, so drift is corrected and failures are retried. The same loop that restarts a crashed pod can recreate a deleted bucket or resize a database.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>This gives platforms three useful properties: a single source of truth for what was asked, automatic repair, and an API that other tools can read, such as the catalog or a pipeline.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"crossplane-in-outline\">Crossplane in outline\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Crossplane extends Kubernetes so that cloud resources are managed as Kubernetes objects. The core ideas are:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Providers\u003c/strong> that know how to talk to a cloud or service and expose its resources as managed resources\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Composite resource definitions\u003c/strong> that define your own API, with a schema\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Compositions\u003c/strong> that map one of your API objects onto several managed resources and decide the defaults\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>A developer writes a short object. The platform team has already decided what it expands into.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: platform.example.org/v1alpha1\nkind: Database\nmetadata:\n  name: orders\n  namespace: team-payments\nspec:\n  engine: postgres\n  size: small\n  environment: dev\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">platform.example.org/v1alpha1\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">kind:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">Database\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">orders\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">namespace:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">team-payments\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">spec:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">engine:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">postgres\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">size:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">small\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">environment:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">dev\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Behind that, a composition might create a database instance, a subnet group, a backup schedule and a secret holding the connection details, with the size mapped to concrete instance classes. Crossplane&#39;s APIs and recommended structure have changed between versions, so use the documentation for the version you run when you write real definitions.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"why-not-give-people-the-cloud-api-directly\">Why not give people the cloud API directly?\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Cloud APIs are wide and powerful. A developer who wants &quot;a small Postgres&quot; would have to choose among dozens of options, any of which might violate policy, cost too much or lack backups. A platform API narrows the choices to the ones the company supports, bakes in encryption, backups, tags and network placement, and gives each request a clear owner and namespace. It also lets you change the implementation, for example moving from one instance type to another, without every team rewriting its configuration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-to-write-an-operator\">When to write an operator\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Compositions cover the common case of mapping one object to several resources. When you need custom logic, such as running a step-by-step migration, calling an internal system or managing a stateful workload, a custom controller or operator is the tool. Frameworks such as Kubebuilder help. Prefer composition when it is enough, because a custom controller is code you must test, run and upgrade.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-the-abstraction-honest\">Keep the abstraction honest\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Abstractions leak. Decide in advance what the user can see and change, and what they cannot. Show the status of the underlying resources in the object&#39;s status, with clear conditions such as \u003ccode>Ready\u003c/code> and a message when something fails. Document the escape hatch for needs the abstraction does not cover. Version the API and plan migrations, because teams will depend on it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"check-a-request-against-supported-sizes\">Check a request against supported sizes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Here is a small stand-in for the logic a composition applies: map a friendly size to concrete settings and refuse anything outside the supported set.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"SIZES = {\n    &quot;small&quot;: {&quot;cpu&quot;: 2, &quot;memory_gb&quot;: 8, &quot;storage_gb&quot;: 50},\n    &quot;medium&quot;: {&quot;cpu&quot;: 4, &quot;memory_gb&quot;: 16, &quot;storage_gb&quot;: 200},\n}\nENGINES = {&quot;postgres&quot;, &quot;mysql&quot;}\n\ndef expand(spec):\n    if spec[&quot;engine&quot;] not in ENGINES:\n        raise ValueError(&quot;engine not supported: &quot; + spec[&quot;engine&quot;])\n    if spec[&quot;size&quot;] not in SIZES:\n        raise ValueError(&quot;size not supported: &quot; + spec[&quot;size&quot;])\n    plan = dict(SIZES[spec[&quot;size&quot;]])\n    plan.update(engine=spec[&quot;engine&quot;], backups=True, encrypted=True, tags={&quot;env&quot;: spec[&quot;environment&quot;]})\n    return plan\n\nprint(expand({&quot;engine&quot;: &quot;postgres&quot;, &quot;size&quot;: &quot;small&quot;, &quot;environment&quot;: &quot;dev&quot;}))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">SIZES = {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;small&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cpu&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;memory_gb&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;storage_gb&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">50\u003c/span>},\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;medium&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cpu&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;memory_gb&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">16\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;storage_gb&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">200\u003c/span>},\n}\nENGINES = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;postgres&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mysql&quot;\u003c/span>}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">expand\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">spec\u003c/span>):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> spec[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;engine&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> ENGINES:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> ValueError(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;engine not supported: &quot;\u003c/span> + spec[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;engine&quot;\u003c/span>])\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> spec[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;size&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> SIZES:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">raise\u003c/span> ValueError(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;size not supported: &quot;\u003c/span> + spec[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;size&quot;\u003c/span>])\n    plan = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">dict\u003c/span>(SIZES[spec[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;size&quot;\u003c/span>]])\n    plan.update(engine=spec[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;engine&quot;\u003c/span>], backups=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>, encrypted=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>, tags={\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;env&quot;\u003c/span>: spec[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;environment&quot;\u003c/span>]})\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> plan\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(expand({\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;engine&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;postgres&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;size&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;small&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;environment&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dev&quot;\u003c/span>}))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"operate-the-control-plane-like-a-product\">Operate the control plane like a product\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The controllers are production software. Monitor reconcile errors and queue depth. Alert on resources stuck not-ready. Back up the control plane state. Plan upgrades of the controllers and providers, and test them against a copy of real resources first. A broken provider upgrade can stall changes everywhere.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"4\" data-edges=\"req&gt;api;api&gt;comp;comp&gt;prov;prov&gt;cloud;cloud&gt;stat;stat&gt;req&gt;visible to user\" style=\"--rows:4\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A request becomes cloud resources\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"90.0\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"87.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"87.5\" x2=\"10.0\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"req\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Database object\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"api\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Platform API\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"comp\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Composition\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prov\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Provider\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"cloud\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:37.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cloud resources\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"stat\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:87.5%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Status\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Database object to Platform API; 2, Platform API to Composition; 3, Composition to Provider; 4, Provider to Cloud resources; 5, Cloud resources to Status; 6, Status to Database object (visible to user).\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"req\">\u003cdt>Database object \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A short request in the team's namespace.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Free-form options that bypass policy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"api\">\u003cdt>Platform API \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Schema, defaults and validation.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Breaking changes without versions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"comp\">\u003cdt>Composition \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Maps the request to managed resources.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Hidden defaults nobody documented.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prov\">\u003cdt>Provider \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Calls the cloud API.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Credentials with too much access.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"cloud\">\u003cdt>Cloud resources \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Instance, backup, subnets and secret.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Drift from manual changes.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"stat\">\u003cdt>Status \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Conditions reported back to the user.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Errors hidden in controller logs.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-ready-but-not-really\">Incident: Ready, but not really\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A team created a Database object, saw it as &quot;created&quot;, and began deploying. The instance never came up because of a quota limit, and the application failed for an hour.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl get database orders -n team-payments\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get database orders -n team-payments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">NAME     READY   SYNCED   AGE\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">orders   False   True     41m\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The object had been accepted, but the readiness condition was false because the provider hit a quota error that only appeared in controller logs. The platform team surfaced the cloud error message in the object&#39;s status, added an alert for resources not ready after ten minutes, and wrote the quota check into the pre-request validation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Exposing the full cloud API instead of a narrow, supported one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Writing a custom controller when a composition would do\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hiding underlying errors from users\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing an API without versioning and a migration path\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving the provider far more cloud permissions than the APIs need\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"declare-then-reconcile","text":"Declare, then reconcile"},{"id":"crossplane-in-outline","text":"Crossplane in outline"},{"id":"why-not-give-people-the-cloud-api-directly","text":"Why not give people the cloud API directly?"},{"id":"when-to-write-an-operator","text":"When to write an operator"},{"id":"keep-the-abstraction-honest","text":"Keep the abstraction honest"},{"id":"check-a-request-against-supported-sizes","text":"Check a request against supported sizes"},{"id":"operate-the-control-plane-like-a-product","text":"Operate the control plane like a product"},{"id":"incident-ready-but-not-really","text":"Incident: Ready, but not really"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Crossplane documentation","u":"https://docs.crossplane.io/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: operator pattern","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/extend-kubernetes/operator/"}],"text":"A form or a pipeline creates things once. A control plane keeps them right. Kubernetes popularised the idea: you declare the state you want, and controllers continuously work to make reality match. Platform teams reuse that pattern to offer their own APIs, such as Database or Environment , and let controllers do the work behind them. What you will learn Why a declarative API suits infrastructure How Crossplane compositions map a simple request to cloud resources When to write an operator or controller instead How to keep the abstraction honest Declare, then reconcile In an imperative script, you tell the system what steps to run. In a declarative API, you say what should exist, and a controller compares desired state with actual state and acts on the difference. It repeats forever, so drift is corrected and failures are retried. The same loop that restarts a crashed pod can recreate a deleted bucket or resize a database. This gives platforms three useful properties: a single source of truth for what was asked, automatic repair, and an API that other tools can read, such as the catalog or a pipeline. Crossplane in outline Crossplane extends Kubernetes so that cloud resources are managed as Kubernetes objects. The core ideas are: Providers that know how to talk to a cloud or service and expose its resources as managed resources Composite resource definitions that define your own API, with a schema Compositions that map one of your API objects onto several managed resources and decide the defaults A developer writes a short object. The platform team has already decided what it expands into. apiVersion: platform.example.org/v1alpha1 kind: Database metadata: name: orders namespace: team-payments spec: engine: postgres size: small environment: dev Behind that, a composition might create a database instance, a subnet group, a backup schedule and a secret holding the connection details, with the size mapped to concrete instance classes. Crossplane&#39;s APIs and recommended structure have changed between versions, so use the documentation for the version you run when you write real definitions. Why not give people the cloud API directly? Cloud APIs are wide and powerful. A developer who wants \"a small Postgres\" would have to choose among dozens of options, any of which might violate policy, cost too much or lack backups. A platform API narrows the choices to the ones the company supports, bakes in encryption, backups, tags and network placement, and gives each request a clear owner and namespace. It also lets you change the implementation, for example moving from one instance type to another, without every team rewriting its configuration. When to write an operator Compositions cover the common case of mapping one object to several resources. When you need custom logic, such as running a step-by-step migration, calling an internal system or managing a stateful workload, a custom controller or operator is the tool. Frameworks such as Kubebuilder help. Prefer composition when it is enough, because a custom controller is code you must test, run and upgrade. Keep the abstraction honest Abstractions leak. Decide in advance what the user can see and change, and what they cannot. Show the status of the underlying resources in the object&#39;s status, with clear conditions such as Ready and a message when something fails. Document the escape hatch for needs the abstraction does not cover. Version the API and plan migrations, because teams will depend on it. Check a request against supported sizes Here is a small stand-in for the logic a composition applies: map a friendly size to concrete settings and refuse anything outside the supported set. SIZES = { \"small\" : { \"cpu\" : 2 , \"memory_gb\" : 8 , \"storage_gb\" : 50 }, \"medium\" : { \"cpu\" : 4 , \"memory_gb\" : 16 , \"storage_gb\" : 200 }, } ENGINES = { \"postgres\" , \"mysql\" } def expand ( spec ): if spec[ \"engine\" ] not in ENGINES: raise ValueError( \"engine not supported: \" + spec[ \"engine\" ]) if spec["},"platform/guardrails-policy-as-code-and-secure-defaults":{"html":"\u003cp>A guardrail keeps people from driving off the road without slowing the journey. In a platform, guardrails are checks and defaults built into the paths and workflows: images must come from an approved registry, services must have an owner, containers must not run as root. When they work, developers rarely notice them. When they are badly designed, they are the thing developers resent most.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Defaults, guardrails and gates, and when to use each\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How policy as code works with admission control\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to write policies that explain themselves\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to roll out a new policy without breaking teams\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"three-levels-of-control\">Three levels of control\u003c/h2>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Defaults.\u003c/strong> The safe option is already chosen. A template sets resource limits and a non-root user. Most people never change them, so the default does most of the work.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Guardrails.\u003c/strong> Automated checks that block or warn on dangerous choices, with a clear message. A policy rejects a deployment with no resource limits.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Gates.\u003c/strong> A human approval for rare, high-risk actions, such as widening access to production data.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Prefer defaults first, guardrails next and gates last. Each step adds friction, so use the lightest control that gives enough safety.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"policy-as-code\">Policy as code\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Policies written in a document are forgotten. Policies written as code can be reviewed, tested, versioned and enforced automatically. In Kubernetes, admission controllers intercept requests to the API server and can validate or mutate them. Tools commonly used for this include Kyverno, Open Policy Agent with Gatekeeper, and the built-in validating admission policies, which use the Common Expression Language. The same rules can also run earlier, in pipelines and in the template step, so developers see failures in their own change instead of at deploy time.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>An example rule, written in plain terms: every Deployment must declare resource requests and limits for each container. In a policy engine this becomes a few lines of configuration. The important part is where it runs and what it says when it fails.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"messages-that-help\">Messages that help\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A policy that rejects with &quot;denied&quot; teaches nothing. A good message says what was wrong, why the rule exists and how to fix it: &quot;Container \u003ccode>web\u003c/code> has no memory limit. Limits stop one service from starving others. Add \u003ccode>resources.limits.memory\u003c/code>, or use the default from the path template.&quot; Link to the documentation. Developers read the message at the moment they are most motivated to fix the problem.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"def check_deployment(d):\n    problems = []\n    for c in d[&quot;containers&quot;]:\n        if &quot;limits&quot; not in c.get(&quot;resources&quot;, {}):\n            problems.append(f&quot;container {c['name']} has no resource limits: add resources.limits so one service cannot starve others&quot;)\n        if c.get(&quot;run_as_root&quot;, True):\n            problems.append(f&quot;container {c['name']} may run as root: set runAsNonRoot to true&quot;)\n    if &quot;owner&quot; not in d.get(&quot;labels&quot;, {}):\n        problems.append(&quot;deployment has no owner label: add the owning team so alerts can be routed&quot;)\n    return problems\n\ndeploy = {\n    &quot;labels&quot;: {},\n    &quot;containers&quot;: [{&quot;name&quot;: &quot;web&quot;, &quot;resources&quot;: {}, &quot;run_as_root&quot;: True}],\n}\nfor p in check_deployment(deploy):\n    print(&quot;DENY:&quot;, p)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">check_deployment\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">d\u003c/span>):\n    problems = []\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> d[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;containers&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;limits&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> c.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;resources&quot;\u003c/span>, {}):\n            problems.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;container \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{c[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;\u003c/span>]}\u003c/span> has no resource limits: add resources.limits so one service cannot starve others&quot;\u003c/span>)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> c.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;run_as_root&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>):\n            problems.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;container \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{c[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;name&#x27;\u003c/span>]}\u003c/span> may run as root: set runAsNonRoot to true&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> d.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels&quot;\u003c/span>, {}):\n        problems.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deployment has no owner label: add the owning team so alerts can be routed&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> problems\n\ndeploy = {\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels&quot;\u003c/span>: {},\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;containers&quot;\u003c/span>: [{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;web&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;resources&quot;\u003c/span>: {}, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;run_as_root&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>}],\n}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> p \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> check_deployment(deploy):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;DENY:&quot;\u003c/span>, p)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"roll-out-in-stages\">Roll out in stages\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Switching a new policy to enforce on day one will break teams and cost you trust. Use stages:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Audit mode: record violations but allow everything\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Publish the list of affected services and the fix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Warn mode: show the message but allow\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enforce, with a dated deadline and a documented exception process\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Measure how many violations remain at each stage. If many teams hit the same violation, improve the template so the default prevents it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"exceptions-are-part-of-the-design\">Exceptions are part of the design\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Real systems have exceptions. Make them explicit, time-limited and visible: an exception has an owner, a reason and an expiry date, and is listed somewhere reviewable. Silent, permanent exceptions erode the policy until it means nothing.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"test-the-policies\">Test the policies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write test cases for each policy, both a passing and a failing example, and run them in CI whenever the policy changes. A policy bug that rejects all deployments is an outage you caused yourself.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"do-not-duplicate-trust\">Do not duplicate trust\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Run the same policy in more than one place only if it is the same code. Different copies drift. Keep one policy repository and let the pipeline, admission controller and template reference it.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"4\" data-edges=\"tmpl&gt;ci;ci&gt;adm;adm&gt;aud;ci&gt;dev&gt;message;adm&gt;dev&gt;message\" style=\"--rows:4\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Guardrails along the path\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"37.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"87.5\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"37.5\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"87.5\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"tmpl\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Template defaults\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ci\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pipeline checks\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"adm\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Admission control\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"aud\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:37.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Audit and exceptions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dev\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:87.5%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Developer\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Template defaults to Pipeline checks; 2, Pipeline checks to Admission control; 3, Admission control to Audit and exceptions; 4, Pipeline checks to Developer (message); 5, Admission control to Developer (message).\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"tmpl\">\u003cdt>Template defaults \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Safe values chosen up front.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Defaults that are rarely updated.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ci\">\u003cdt>Pipeline checks \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Policy and scan results in the pull request.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Checks that can be bypassed.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"adm\">\u003cdt>Admission control \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Validates requests to the cluster.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A rule that blocks everything.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"aud\">\u003cdt>Audit and exceptions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Violations and time-limited exceptions.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Permanent silent exceptions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dev\">\u003cdt>Developer \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Sees clear messages and fixes the cause.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Messages that only say denied.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-rule-that-blocked-a-rollback\">Incident: The rule that blocked a rollback\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>During an outage the on-call engineer tried to roll back a deployment, and an admission policy rejected the old version because it lacked a label added after it was built.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"kubectl rollout undo deployment/checkout -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl rollout undo deployment/checkout -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error from server: admission webhook &quot;policy.platform&quot; denied the request: missing label owner\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The new policy applied to old workloads as well as new ones. The incident lasted fifteen minutes longer while a platform engineer granted an exception. Afterwards the team made new policies apply only to resources created or changed after a date, added an emergency path with logging for rollbacks, and tested policies against old versions before enforcing them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Relying on documents and reviews instead of automated checks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Denying with a message that gives no fix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Enforcing a new policy on day one\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting exceptions live forever without owners\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running different copies of the same policy in different places\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"three-levels-of-control","text":"Three levels of control"},{"id":"policy-as-code","text":"Policy as code"},{"id":"messages-that-help","text":"Messages that help"},{"id":"roll-out-in-stages","text":"Roll out in stages"},{"id":"exceptions-are-part-of-the-design","text":"Exceptions are part of the design"},{"id":"test-the-policies","text":"Test the policies"},{"id":"do-not-duplicate-trust","text":"Do not duplicate trust"},{"id":"incident-the-rule-that-blocked-a-rollback","text":"Incident: The rule that blocked a rollback"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: validating admission policy","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/access-authn-authz/validating-admission-policy/"},{"t":"Kyverno documentation","u":"https://kyverno.io/docs/"}],"text":"A guardrail keeps people from driving off the road without slowing the journey. In a platform, guardrails are checks and defaults built into the paths and workflows: images must come from an approved registry, services must have an owner, containers must not run as root. When they work, developers rarely notice them. When they are badly designed, they are the thing developers resent most. What you will learn Defaults, guardrails and gates, and when to use each How policy as code works with admission control How to write policies that explain themselves How to roll out a new policy without breaking teams Three levels of control Defaults. The safe option is already chosen. A template sets resource limits and a non-root user. Most people never change them, so the default does most of the work. Guardrails. Automated checks that block or warn on dangerous choices, with a clear message. A policy rejects a deployment with no resource limits. Gates. A human approval for rare, high-risk actions, such as widening access to production data. Prefer defaults first, guardrails next and gates last. Each step adds friction, so use the lightest control that gives enough safety. Policy as code Policies written in a document are forgotten. Policies written as code can be reviewed, tested, versioned and enforced automatically. In Kubernetes, admission controllers intercept requests to the API server and can validate or mutate them. Tools commonly used for this include Kyverno, Open Policy Agent with Gatekeeper, and the built-in validating admission policies, which use the Common Expression Language. The same rules can also run earlier, in pipelines and in the template step, so developers see failures in their own change instead of at deploy time. An example rule, written in plain terms: every Deployment must declare resource requests and limits for each container. In a policy engine this becomes a few lines of configuration. The important part is where it runs and what it says when it fails. Messages that help A policy that rejects with \"denied\" teaches nothing. A good message says what was wrong, why the rule exists and how to fix it: \"Container web has no memory limit. Limits stop one service from starving others. Add resources.limits.memory , or use the default from the path template.\" Link to the documentation. Developers read the message at the moment they are most motivated to fix the problem. def check_deployment ( d ): problems = [] for c in d[ \"containers\" ]: if \"limits\" not in c.get( \"resources\" , {}): problems.append( f\"container {c[ &#x27;name&#x27; ]} has no resource limits: add resources.limits so one service cannot starve others\" ) if c.get( \"run_as_root\" , True ): problems.append( f\"container {c[ &#x27;name&#x27; ]} may run as root: set runAsNonRoot to true\" ) if \"owner\" not in d.get( \"labels\" , {}): problems.append( \"deployment has no owner label: add the owning team so alerts can be routed\" ) return problems deploy = { \"labels\" : {}, \"containers\" : [{ \"name\" : \"web\" , \"resources\" : {}, \"run_as_root\" : True }], } for p in check_deployment(deploy): print ( \"DENY:\" , p) Roll out in stages Switching a new policy to enforce on day one will break teams and cost you trust. Use stages: Audit mode: record violations but allow everything Publish the list of affected services and the fix Warn mode: show the message but allow Enforce, with a dated deadline and a documented exception process Measure how many violations remain at each stage. If many teams hit the same violation, improve the template so the default prevents it. Exceptions are part of the design Real systems have exceptions. Make them explicit, time-limited and visible: an exception has an owner, a reason and an expiry date, and is listed somewhere reviewable. Silent, permanent exceptions erode the policy until it means nothing. Test the policies Write test cases for each policy, both a passing and a failing example, and run them in CI whenever the policy changes. A policy bug "},"platform/environments-ephemeral-previews-and-multi-tenancy":{"html":"\u003cp>Teams need somewhere to run their code before production. Too few environments and people step on each other. Too many and the cloud bill and the maintenance work balloon. A platform decides how environments are shared, how isolated they are and how they are created and removed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Choices for sharing clusters between teams\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How namespaces, quotas and policies create soft isolation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ephemeral preview environments for each change\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Promotion paths and cleanup\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"shared-or-separate\">Shared or separate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The main choices, from cheapest to most isolated, are:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Model\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Isolation\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Cost and effort\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Namespace per team on a shared cluster\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Soft, enforced by policy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Lowest\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Virtual clusters per team\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Separate API server, shared nodes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Medium\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cluster per team or per environment\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Hard, separate control planes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Highest\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Most platforms start with namespaces and move teams to stronger isolation when risk or scale demands it, for example for regulated workloads. The Kubernetes documentation on multi-tenancy describes the control plane and data plane concerns for each model.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"making-namespaces-behave-like-tenants\">Making namespaces behave like tenants\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A namespace by itself is only a naming boundary. To make it a tenant, add:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>RBAC\u003c/strong> so a team can manage only its own namespaces\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Resource quotas and limit ranges\u003c/strong> so one team cannot consume the whole cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Network policies\u003c/strong> with default deny between namespaces, and explicit allows\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Pod security standards\u003c/strong> to restrict dangerous pod settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Default labels\u003c/strong> for owner and cost allocation\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The platform should create all of this when a team onboards, from one definition, so every tenant starts identical.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"ephemeral-preview-environments\">Ephemeral preview environments\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A preview environment is a short-lived copy of the application created for a pull request, reachable on its own URL, and deleted when the pull request closes. Reviewers and testers see the change running. Compared with a shared staging environment, previews remove the queue for &quot;who is using staging&quot; and catch integration problems earlier.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>To make previews practical:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Keep them small: use reduced replicas and small databases, or seed data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Create them from the same manifests as other environments, with parameters\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Give them a time limit and delete them automatically\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Isolate their data, and never copy production data without masking\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Tag every resource with the pull request and owner so cost and cleanup are traceable\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"promotion-paths\">Promotion paths\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Define how a change moves from development to production: the stages, the checks at each, and what is different. Avoid environment-specific code. Differences should be configuration, not branches. A change should be the same artifact at every stage, so what you tested is what you ship.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cleanup-is-a-feature\">Cleanup is a feature\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Forgotten environments are one of the largest sources of wasted spend and a security risk, because old environments are rarely patched. Run an automatic job that finds environments past their expiry or without an owner and removes them, with a warning first.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"from datetime import date, timedelta\n\ntoday = date(2026, 11, 14)\nenvs = [\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;pr-412&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;team-search&quot;, &quot;expires&quot;: date(2026, 11, 16)},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;pr-377&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;team-search&quot;, &quot;expires&quot;: date(2026, 10, 30)},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;demo-old&quot;, &quot;owner&quot;: None, &quot;expires&quot;: None},\n]\n\nfor e in envs:\n    if not e[&quot;owner&quot;] or not e[&quot;expires&quot;]:\n        print(e[&quot;name&quot;], &quot;no owner or expiry: warn and schedule removal&quot;)\n    elif e[&quot;expires&quot;] &lt; today:\n        print(e[&quot;name&quot;], &quot;expired&quot;, (today - e[&quot;expires&quot;]).days, &quot;days ago: delete&quot;)\n    elif e[&quot;expires&quot;] - today &lt; timedelta(days=3):\n        print(e[&quot;name&quot;], &quot;expires soon: notify owner&quot;)\n    else:\n        print(e[&quot;name&quot;], &quot;ok&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> datetime \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> date, timedelta\n\ntoday = date(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2026\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">11\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">14\u003c/span>)\nenvs = [\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pr-412&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team-search&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expires&quot;\u003c/span>: date(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2026\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">11\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">16\u003c/span>)},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;pr-377&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team-search&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expires&quot;\u003c/span>: date(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2026\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>)},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;demo-old&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expires&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">None\u003c/span>},\n]\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> e \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> envs:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">or\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expires&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;no owner or expiry: warn and schedule removal&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">elif\u003c/span> e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expires&quot;\u003c/span>] &lt; today:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expired&quot;\u003c/span>, (today - e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expires&quot;\u003c/span>]).days, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;days ago: delete&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">elif\u003c/span> e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expires&quot;\u003c/span>] - today &lt; timedelta(days=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>):\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;expires soon: notify owner&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span>:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(e[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ok&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"when-soft-isolation-is-not-enough\">When soft isolation is not enough\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Namespaces share a kernel, nodes and often a control plane. If tenants are untrusted, handle sensitive data or must meet strict compliance, use stronger isolation: separate node pools, sandboxed runtimes, virtual clusters or separate clusters. Decide this from a threat model, not from convenience.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"onb&gt;ns;ns&gt;pr;ns&gt;stg;pr&gt;prod;stg&gt;prod;gc&gt;pr;gc&gt;stg\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">A tenant and its environments\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"90.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"90.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"onb\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Team onboarding\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"ns\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Namespaces and quotas\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pr\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Preview environments\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"stg\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Shared staging\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"prod\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Production\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gc\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cleanup job\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Team onboarding to Namespaces and quotas; 2, Namespaces and quotas to Preview environments; 3, Namespaces and quotas to Shared staging; 4, Preview environments to Production; 5, Shared staging to Production; 6, Cleanup job to Preview environments; 7, Cleanup job to Shared staging.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"onb\">\u003cdt>Team onboarding \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One definition creates the tenant.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Manual setup that differs per team.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"ns\">\u003cdt>Namespaces and quotas \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Isolation, limits and default labels.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing network policy.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pr\">\u003cdt>Preview environments \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> One per pull request, deleted on close.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Never cleaned up.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"stg\">\u003cdt>Shared staging \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Integration tests before release.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Everyone fights for it.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"prod\">\u003cdt>Production \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Same artifact, different configuration.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Environment-specific code.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gc\">\u003cdt>Cleanup job \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Removes expired or ownerless environments.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Not running, or too gentle.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-preview-that-outlived-the-pull-request\">Incident: The preview that outlived the pull request\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A cloud bill jumped by 18 percent over two months. The cause was hundreds of preview environments that nobody deleted.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"platformctl envs --older-than 14d --count\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>platformctl envs --older-than 14d --count\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">preview environments older than 14d: 312\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">estimated monthly cost: 9,400\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">owners missing: 41\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The preview workflow created environments on pull request open but relied on a webhook to delete them on close, and the webhook silently failed for merged branches. The team added a time-to-live label to every preview, a nightly sweep that deleted anything expired, and a weekly report of the count and cost, so the next leak would show up in days.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Treating a namespace as a security boundary without quotas, policies and RBAC\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Creating environments by hand with different settings\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Allowing previews with no expiry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Copying unmasked production data into test environments\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using code branches instead of configuration to handle environment differences\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"shared-or-separate","text":"Shared or separate"},{"id":"making-namespaces-behave-like-tenants","text":"Making namespaces behave like tenants"},{"id":"ephemeral-preview-environments","text":"Ephemeral preview environments"},{"id":"promotion-paths","text":"Promotion paths"},{"id":"cleanup-is-a-feature","text":"Cleanup is a feature"},{"id":"when-soft-isolation-is-not-enough","text":"When soft isolation is not enough"},{"id":"incident-the-preview-that-outlived-the-pull-request","text":"Incident: The preview that outlived the pull request"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Kubernetes: multi-tenancy","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/multi-tenancy/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: resource quotas","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/policy/resource-quotas/"}],"text":"Teams need somewhere to run their code before production. Too few environments and people step on each other. Too many and the cloud bill and the maintenance work balloon. A platform decides how environments are shared, how isolated they are and how they are created and removed. What you will learn Choices for sharing clusters between teams How namespaces, quotas and policies create soft isolation Ephemeral preview environments for each change Promotion paths and cleanup Shared or separate The main choices, from cheapest to most isolated, are: Model Isolation Cost and effort Namespace per team on a shared cluster Soft, enforced by policy Lowest Virtual clusters per team Separate API server, shared nodes Medium Cluster per team or per environment Hard, separate control planes Highest Most platforms start with namespaces and move teams to stronger isolation when risk or scale demands it, for example for regulated workloads. The Kubernetes documentation on multi-tenancy describes the control plane and data plane concerns for each model. Making namespaces behave like tenants A namespace by itself is only a naming boundary. To make it a tenant, add: RBAC so a team can manage only its own namespaces Resource quotas and limit ranges so one team cannot consume the whole cluster Network policies with default deny between namespaces, and explicit allows Pod security standards to restrict dangerous pod settings Default labels for owner and cost allocation The platform should create all of this when a team onboards, from one definition, so every tenant starts identical. Ephemeral preview environments A preview environment is a short-lived copy of the application created for a pull request, reachable on its own URL, and deleted when the pull request closes. Reviewers and testers see the change running. Compared with a shared staging environment, previews remove the queue for \"who is using staging\" and catch integration problems earlier. To make previews practical: Keep them small: use reduced replicas and small databases, or seed data Create them from the same manifests as other environments, with parameters Give them a time limit and delete them automatically Isolate their data, and never copy production data without masking Tag every resource with the pull request and owner so cost and cleanup are traceable Promotion paths Define how a change moves from development to production: the stages, the checks at each, and what is different. Avoid environment-specific code. Differences should be configuration, not branches. A change should be the same artifact at every stage, so what you tested is what you ship. Cleanup is a feature Forgotten environments are one of the largest sources of wasted spend and a security risk, because old environments are rarely patched. Run an automatic job that finds environments past their expiry or without an owner and removes them, with a warning first. from datetime import date, timedelta today = date( 2026 , 11 , 14 ) envs = [ { \"name\" : \"pr-412\" , \"owner\" : \"team-search\" , \"expires\" : date( 2026 , 11 , 16 )}, { \"name\" : \"pr-377\" , \"owner\" : \"team-search\" , \"expires\" : date( 2026 , 10 , 30 )}, { \"name\" : \"demo-old\" , \"owner\" : None , \"expires\" : None }, ] for e in envs: if not e[ \"owner\" ] or not e[ \"expires\" ]: print (e[ \"name\" ], \"no owner or expiry: warn and schedule removal\" ) elif e[ \"expires\" ] \u003c today: print (e[ \"name\" ], \"expired\" , (today - e[ \"expires\" ]).days, \"days ago: delete\" ) elif e[ \"expires\" ] - today \u003c timedelta(days= 3 ): print (e[ \"name\" ], \"expires soon: notify owner\" ) else : print (e[ \"name\" ], \"ok\" ) When soft isolation is not enough Namespaces share a kernel, nodes and often a control plane. If tenants are untrusted, handle sensitive data or must meet strict compliance, use stronger isolation: separate node pools, sandboxed runtimes, virtual clusters or separate clusters. Decide this from a threat model, not from convenience. A tenant and its environments Flow in order: 1, Team onboar"},"platform/cost-allocation-showback-and-unit-economics":{"html":"\u003cp>Shared platforms hide cost. When twenty teams run on one cluster, the bill arrives as a single number, and nobody feels responsible for it. Cost allocation breaks the bill down to the teams and services that caused it, and gives them data they can act on. It is a core platform capability because only the platform can label everything consistently from the start.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How to label resources so cost can be attributed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to split shared and idle costs fairly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The difference between showback and chargeback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to express cost per unit of product value\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"labels-first\">Labels first\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Allocation depends on consistent labels or tags on cloud resources and on Kubernetes workloads: owner team, service, environment and, where relevant, cost centre. The platform should apply these automatically from the catalog, as part of templates and policies, and reject resources without them. Retrofitting labels is slow and always incomplete.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Cloud providers support cost allocation tags in their billing tools. For Kubernetes, tools such as OpenCost, a CNCF project, estimate cost per namespace, workload or label by combining resource usage with node prices. The FinOps Foundation publishes a framework of practices and terms for this area.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"direct-and-shared-costs\">Direct and shared costs\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some costs belong clearly to one service: its database, its storage. Others are shared: the cluster control plane, networking, monitoring, the platform team&#39;s own tools. You need a rule for splitting the shared part. Common choices are proportional to usage, such as CPU and memory requested, equal shares, or a fixed percentage. Pick one, publish it, and apply it consistently. A rule people can understand is better than a perfect but obscure one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"idle-and-unallocated-cost\">Idle and unallocated cost\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Reserved capacity that nobody is using still costs money. Track it as its own line: idle node capacity, unattached disks, empty reserved instances. If you hide it by spreading it evenly, no one has a reason to reduce it. Showing it separately lets the platform team own the efficiency of the shared layer.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"showback-and-chargeback\">Showback and chargeback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Showback\u003c/strong> shows each team the cost it caused, without moving money. It informs and builds awareness.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Chargeback\u003c/strong> actually bills the team&#39;s budget. It creates stronger incentives but also arguments over the splitting rules, so you need accurate data first.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Most organisations start with showback and move to chargeback only when the data is trusted and there is a business reason. Either way, give teams a monthly view they can drill into, with trends and anomalies.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-simple-allocation\">A simple allocation\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The snippet below splits a shared cluster bill by requested CPU and shows the unallocated portion.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"cluster_cost = 12000.0\nrequested_cpu = {&quot;payments&quot;: 120, &quot;search&quot;: 80, &quot;checkout&quot;: 40}\nnode_cpu_total = 400  # total capacity, including unused\n\nallocated = sum(requested_cpu.values())\nunit = cluster_cost / node_cpu_total\nfor team, cpu in requested_cpu.items():\n    print(f&quot;{team:10} {cpu * unit:8.2f}&quot;)\nidle = (node_cpu_total - allocated) * unit\nprint(f&quot;{'idle':10} {idle:8.2f}&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">cluster_cost = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">12000.0\u003c/span>\nrequested_cpu = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;payments&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">120\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;search&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;checkout&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">40\u003c/span>}\nnode_cpu_total = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">400\u003c/span>  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># total capacity, including unused\u003c/span>\n\nallocated = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(requested_cpu.values())\nunit = cluster_cost / node_cpu_total\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> team, cpu \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> requested_cpu.items():\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{team:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{cpu * unit:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8.2\u003c/span>f}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\nidle = (node_cpu_total - allocated) * unit\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;idle&#x27;\u003c/span>:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{idle:\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8.2\u003c/span>f}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Here costs are split by requested capacity against total capacity, so idle capacity appears as its own line.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"unit-economics\">Unit economics\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Total cost does not say whether spend is healthy. A team whose cost doubled because traffic tripled is doing well. Express cost per unit of value: per thousand requests, per active user, per build, per gigabyte processed. Plot it over time. A rising unit cost is a signal to investigate; a falling one shows efficiency gains. Choose a unit that the product team recognises.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"give-actionable-advice\">Give actionable advice\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Reports that only show numbers are ignored. Pair data with suggestions: right-size requests that are consistently far above usage, remove idle environments, move batch work to cheaper capacity, and use autoscaling. But protect reliability: do not recommend cuts that remove headroom for production traffic.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-the-platform-s-own-cost-visible\">Keep the platform&#39;s own cost visible\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The platform also costs money. Report what it costs per supported team or per deploy, and what it saves. This keeps the conversation about value, not only about spend.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"bill&gt;alloc;label&gt;alloc;usage&gt;alloc;alloc&gt;view;view&gt;act\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">From bill to team view\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bill\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Cloud bill\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"label\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Labels and tags\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"usage\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Usage data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"alloc\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Allocation rules\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"view\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Team cost view\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"act\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Actions\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Cloud bill to Allocation rules; 2, Labels and tags to Allocation rules; 3, Usage data to Allocation rules; 4, Allocation rules to Team cost view; 5, Team cost view to Actions.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bill\">\u003cdt>Cloud bill \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Raw cost by account and service.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Single unexplained total.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"label\">\u003cdt>Labels and tags \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Owner, service and environment.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Missing or inconsistent labels.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"usage\">\u003cdt>Usage data \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Requests and consumption per workload.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No usage data, only totals.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"alloc\">\u003cdt>Allocation rules \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Direct, shared and idle costs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rules nobody understands.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"view\">\u003cdt>Team cost view \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Showback with trends and unit costs.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Numbers with no advice.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"act\">\u003cdt>Actions \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Right-size, clean up and plan.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Cutting reliability headroom.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-unlabelled-third\">Incident: The unlabelled third\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Finance asked which team caused a 30 percent rise in monthly spend. The platform team could attribute only two thirds of the bill.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"costctl report --month 2026-10 --group-by team\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>costctl report --month 2026-10 --group-by team\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">payments        41,200\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">search          28,900\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">checkout        19,400\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">unallocated     36,300\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A large share of resources had been created before labelling was required, and several automation scripts created resources without tags. The team added a policy that denied unlabelled resources, back-filled labels from the catalog using resource names, and reported unallocated spend every week as a number the platform team had to reduce.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Starting allocation after resources already exist without labels\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Hiding idle capacity inside team bills\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using chargeback before the data is trusted\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Reporting only total cost, not cost per unit of value\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Recommending savings that remove production headroom\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"labels-first","text":"Labels first"},{"id":"direct-and-shared-costs","text":"Direct and shared costs"},{"id":"idle-and-unallocated-cost","text":"Idle and unallocated cost"},{"id":"showback-and-chargeback","text":"Showback and chargeback"},{"id":"a-simple-allocation","text":"A simple allocation"},{"id":"unit-economics","text":"Unit economics"},{"id":"give-actionable-advice","text":"Give actionable advice"},{"id":"keep-the-platform-s-own-cost-visible","text":"Keep the platform's own cost visible"},{"id":"incident-the-unlabelled-third","text":"Incident: The unlabelled third"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"FinOps Foundation framework","u":"https://www.finops.org/framework/"},{"t":"OpenCost documentation","u":"https://opencost.io/docs/"}],"text":"Shared platforms hide cost. When twenty teams run on one cluster, the bill arrives as a single number, and nobody feels responsible for it. Cost allocation breaks the bill down to the teams and services that caused it, and gives them data they can act on. It is a core platform capability because only the platform can label everything consistently from the start. What you will learn How to label resources so cost can be attributed How to split shared and idle costs fairly The difference between showback and chargeback How to express cost per unit of product value Labels first Allocation depends on consistent labels or tags on cloud resources and on Kubernetes workloads: owner team, service, environment and, where relevant, cost centre. The platform should apply these automatically from the catalog, as part of templates and policies, and reject resources without them. Retrofitting labels is slow and always incomplete. Cloud providers support cost allocation tags in their billing tools. For Kubernetes, tools such as OpenCost, a CNCF project, estimate cost per namespace, workload or label by combining resource usage with node prices. The FinOps Foundation publishes a framework of practices and terms for this area. Direct and shared costs Some costs belong clearly to one service: its database, its storage. Others are shared: the cluster control plane, networking, monitoring, the platform team&#39;s own tools. You need a rule for splitting the shared part. Common choices are proportional to usage, such as CPU and memory requested, equal shares, or a fixed percentage. Pick one, publish it, and apply it consistently. A rule people can understand is better than a perfect but obscure one. Idle and unallocated cost Reserved capacity that nobody is using still costs money. Track it as its own line: idle node capacity, unattached disks, empty reserved instances. If you hide it by spreading it evenly, no one has a reason to reduce it. Showing it separately lets the platform team own the efficiency of the shared layer. Showback and chargeback Showback shows each team the cost it caused, without moving money. It informs and builds awareness. Chargeback actually bills the team&#39;s budget. It creates stronger incentives but also arguments over the splitting rules, so you need accurate data first. Most organisations start with showback and move to chargeback only when the data is trusted and there is a business reason. Either way, give teams a monthly view they can drill into, with trends and anomalies. A simple allocation The snippet below splits a shared cluster bill by requested CPU and shows the unallocated portion. cluster_cost = 12000.0 requested_cpu = { \"payments\" : 120 , \"search\" : 80 , \"checkout\" : 40 } node_cpu_total = 400 # total capacity, including unused allocated = sum (requested_cpu.values()) unit = cluster_cost / node_cpu_total for team, cpu in requested_cpu.items(): print ( f\" {team: 10 } {cpu * unit: 8.2 f} \" ) idle = (node_cpu_total - allocated) * unit print ( f\" { &#x27;idle&#x27; : 10 } {idle: 8.2 f} \" ) Here costs are split by requested capacity against total capacity, so idle capacity appears as its own line. Unit economics Total cost does not say whether spend is healthy. A team whose cost doubled because traffic tripled is doing well. Express cost per unit of value: per thousand requests, per active user, per build, per gigabyte processed. Plot it over time. A rising unit cost is a signal to investigate; a falling one shows efficiency gains. Choose a unit that the product team recognises. Give actionable advice Reports that only show numbers are ignored. Pair data with suggestions: right-size requests that are consistently far above usage, remove idle environments, move batch work to cheaper capacity, and use autoscaling. But protect reliability: do not recommend cuts that remove headroom for production traffic. Keep the platform&#39;s own cost visible The platform also costs money. Report what it costs per supported t"},"platform/measuring-developer-experience-dora-space-and-cognitive-load":{"html":"\u003cp>A platform team that cannot show whether it helps will lose its budget to the first reorganisation. A platform team that measures the wrong things will optimise for the wrong outcome. The aim is a few measures that tell you whether developers can deliver changes safely and with less pain, and that you cannot easily game.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The DORA software delivery measures and how to collect them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>The dimensions of the SPACE framework\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Onboarding time and survey questions that reveal friction\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Common traps when measuring people and teams\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"dora-delivery-measures\">DORA delivery measures\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The DevOps Research and Assessment programme studied thousands of teams and found a small set of measures that relate to delivery performance:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Deployment frequency\u003c/strong>: how often a team releases to production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Lead time for changes\u003c/strong>: how long from a commit to running in production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Change failure rate\u003c/strong>: the share of releases that cause a failure needing a fix\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Time to restore\u003c/strong>: how quickly service recovers after a failed release or incident\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Newer reports refine the names and add related measures, so check the current definitions at dora.dev. The first two describe speed and the last two describe stability. The research finding that matters most is that speed and stability tend to rise together, not trade off, in high performing teams.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>You can compute these from data the platform already holds: deployment events from the pipeline, commit times from source control, and incident or rollback records. Measure them per service or team, not as a company-wide average that hides who needs help.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"from datetime import datetime\nfrom statistics import median\n\ndeploys = [\n    {&quot;commit&quot;: datetime(2026, 11, 2, 9, 0), &quot;live&quot;: datetime(2026, 11, 2, 11, 30), &quot;failed&quot;: False},\n    {&quot;commit&quot;: datetime(2026, 11, 3, 14, 0), &quot;live&quot;: datetime(2026, 11, 3, 15, 0), &quot;failed&quot;: True},\n    {&quot;commit&quot;: datetime(2026, 11, 5, 10, 0), &quot;live&quot;: datetime(2026, 11, 5, 16, 0), &quot;failed&quot;: False},\n    {&quot;commit&quot;: datetime(2026, 11, 9, 8, 0), &quot;live&quot;: datetime(2026, 11, 9, 8, 45), &quot;failed&quot;: False},\n]\ndays = 14\n\nlead_hours = [(d[&quot;live&quot;] - d[&quot;commit&quot;]).total_seconds() / 3600 for d in deploys]\nprint(&quot;deploys per week:&quot;, round(len(deploys) / (days / 7), 1))\nprint(&quot;median lead time (hours):&quot;, round(median(lead_hours), 1))\nprint(&quot;change failure rate:&quot;, sum(d[&quot;failed&quot;] for d in deploys) / len(deploys))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> datetime \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> datetime\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">from\u003c/span> statistics \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> median\n\ndeploys = [\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;commit&quot;\u003c/span>: datetime(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2026\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">11\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">9\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;live&quot;\u003c/span>: datetime(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2026\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">11\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">11\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;failed&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;commit&quot;\u003c/span>: datetime(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2026\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">11\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">14\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;live&quot;\u003c/span>: datetime(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2026\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">11\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">15\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;failed&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">True\u003c/span>},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;commit&quot;\u003c/span>: datetime(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2026\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">11\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;live&quot;\u003c/span>: datetime(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2026\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">11\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">5\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">16\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;failed&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;commit&quot;\u003c/span>: datetime(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2026\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">11\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">9\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;live&quot;\u003c/span>: datetime(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2026\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">11\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">9\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">8\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">45\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;failed&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>},\n]\ndays = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">14\u003c/span>\n\nlead_hours = [(d[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;live&quot;\u003c/span>] - d[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;commit&quot;\u003c/span>]).total_seconds() / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3600\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> d \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> deploys]\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;deploys per week:&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">round\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(deploys) / (days / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">7\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>))\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;median lead time (hours):&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">round\u003c/span>(median(lead_hours), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>))\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;change failure rate:&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum\u003c/span>(d[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;failed&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> d \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> deploys) / \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">len\u003c/span>(deploys))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-space-framework\">The SPACE framework\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Researchers from Microsoft, GitHub and the University of Victoria proposed SPACE as a way to think about developer productivity beyond one number. It covers five dimensions:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Satisfaction and well-being\u003c/strong>: how developers feel about their work and tools\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Performance\u003c/strong>: outcomes such as quality and impact\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Activity\u003c/strong>: counts of actions such as commits or deploys\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Communication and collaboration\u003c/strong>: how well people and teams work together\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Efficiency and flow\u003c/strong>: how smoothly work proceeds with few interruptions and waits\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>The core advice is to measure across several dimensions, and to include at least one perception measure. Activity counts alone, such as lines of code or number of commits, are easy to collect and easy to game.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"cognitive-load-and-onboarding\">Cognitive load and onboarding\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Two measures speak directly to what a platform does. \u003cstrong>Time to first deploy\u003c/strong> for a new engineer, from laptop setup to a change running in a real environment, shows how much the platform removes. \u003cstrong>Time to create a new service\u003c/strong> shows the cost of starting something. Track both as medians, and run a short survey after each task asking how hard it was and what slowed the person down.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>A useful survey is brief and recurring: how easy is it to deploy, to debug in production, to find documentation, to get help? Add a free-text question. A rating alone tells you that something is wrong; the comment tells you what.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"treat-adoption-as-evidence-not-proof\">Treat adoption as evidence, not proof\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Rising usage of the platform is good news but not proof of value, especially if it is mandatory. Pair it with satisfaction and outcome measures. If usage is high and satisfaction is low, people are tolerating it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"traps-to-avoid\">Traps to avoid\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Targets become goals.\u003c/strong> When a measure becomes a target, people optimise the number. Use measures to learn and discuss, not to rank individuals.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Comparing teams unfairly.\u003c/strong> A team maintaining a regulated mainframe interface has different constraints from a new web service.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Averages that hide pain.\u003c/strong> Use medians and percentiles, and look at the slowest cases.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Measuring only what is easy.\u003c/strong> Deploy counts are easy; the feeling of being blocked is not, and it often matters more.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Surveillance.\u003c/strong> Individual tracking harms trust. Measure systems and teams.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"close-the-loop\">Close the loop\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Review the measures with the product teams every quarter. Pick one or two to improve, make a hypothesis about what platform change will move them, and check afterwards. The conversation is the product of measurement, not the dashboard.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"data&gt;dora;data&gt;onb;dora&gt;rev;onb&gt;rev;survey&gt;rev;rev&gt;chg\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Measuring whether the platform helps\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"data\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Platform data\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dora\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Delivery measures\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"onb\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Onboarding time\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"survey\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Developer survey\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"rev\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Quarterly review\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"chg\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Platform change\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Platform data to Delivery measures; 2, Platform data to Onboarding time; 3, Delivery measures to Quarterly review; 4, Onboarding time to Quarterly review; 5, Developer survey to Quarterly review; 6, Quarterly review to Platform change.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"data\">\u003cdt>Platform data \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Deploy events, commits and incidents.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Data stored per tool and never joined.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dora\">\u003cdt>Delivery measures \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Frequency, lead time, failure rate and recovery.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Company-wide averages hiding outliers.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"onb\">\u003cdt>Onboarding time \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Time to first deploy and to a new service.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Measured once, never repeated.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"survey\">\u003cdt>Developer survey \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Ease, blockers and free text.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Rare surveys with no follow-up.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"rev\">\u003cdt>Quarterly review \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pick one or two things to improve.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Dashboards without decisions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"chg\">\u003cdt>Platform change \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> A hypothesis tested against the measures.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Changes made without a baseline.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-leaderboard\">Incident: The leaderboard\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A manager published a ranking of teams by deployment count. Within a month, teams were splitting changes into tiny deploys, and change failure rate did not improve.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"platformctl metrics --team search --last 30d\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>platformctl metrics --team search --last 30d\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">deploys:            94   (previous 30d: 41)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">median lead time:   2.9h (previous: 3.1h)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">change failure:     17%  (previous: 16%)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">rollbacks:          15   (previous: 7)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Deployment frequency doubled but the stability numbers were unchanged or worse, which is a sign of gaming. The platform team withdrew the leaderboard, replaced it with per-team trends that only the team and its manager saw, and added the survey question &quot;what slows you down most?&quot; to the quarterly review.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Ranking individuals or teams by a single activity count\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using a company-wide average that hides problem teams\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the human side: surveys and free-text feedback\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Treating mandatory usage as success\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Changing the platform with no baseline to compare against\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"dora-delivery-measures","text":"DORA delivery measures"},{"id":"the-space-framework","text":"The SPACE framework"},{"id":"cognitive-load-and-onboarding","text":"Cognitive load and onboarding"},{"id":"treat-adoption-as-evidence-not-proof","text":"Treat adoption as evidence, not proof"},{"id":"traps-to-avoid","text":"Traps to avoid"},{"id":"close-the-loop","text":"Close the loop"},{"id":"incident-the-leaderboard","text":"Incident: The leaderboard"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"DORA research programme","u":"https://dora.dev/"},{"t":"Team Topologies key concepts","u":"https://teamtopologies.com/key-concepts"}],"text":"A platform team that cannot show whether it helps will lose its budget to the first reorganisation. A platform team that measures the wrong things will optimise for the wrong outcome. The aim is a few measures that tell you whether developers can deliver changes safely and with less pain, and that you cannot easily game. What you will learn The DORA software delivery measures and how to collect them The dimensions of the SPACE framework Onboarding time and survey questions that reveal friction Common traps when measuring people and teams DORA delivery measures The DevOps Research and Assessment programme studied thousands of teams and found a small set of measures that relate to delivery performance: Deployment frequency : how often a team releases to production Lead time for changes : how long from a commit to running in production Change failure rate : the share of releases that cause a failure needing a fix Time to restore : how quickly service recovers after a failed release or incident Newer reports refine the names and add related measures, so check the current definitions at dora.dev. The first two describe speed and the last two describe stability. The research finding that matters most is that speed and stability tend to rise together, not trade off, in high performing teams. You can compute these from data the platform already holds: deployment events from the pipeline, commit times from source control, and incident or rollback records. Measure them per service or team, not as a company-wide average that hides who needs help. from datetime import datetime from statistics import median deploys = [ { \"commit\" : datetime( 2026 , 11 , 2 , 9 , 0 ), \"live\" : datetime( 2026 , 11 , 2 , 11 , 30 ), \"failed\" : False }, { \"commit\" : datetime( 2026 , 11 , 3 , 14 , 0 ), \"live\" : datetime( 2026 , 11 , 3 , 15 , 0 ), \"failed\" : True }, { \"commit\" : datetime( 2026 , 11 , 5 , 10 , 0 ), \"live\" : datetime( 2026 , 11 , 5 , 16 , 0 ), \"failed\" : False }, { \"commit\" : datetime( 2026 , 11 , 9 , 8 , 0 ), \"live\" : datetime( 2026 , 11 , 9 , 8 , 45 ), \"failed\" : False }, ] days = 14 lead_hours = [(d[ \"live\" ] - d[ \"commit\" ]).total_seconds() / 3600 for d in deploys] print ( \"deploys per week:\" , round ( len (deploys) / (days / 7 ), 1 )) print ( \"median lead time (hours):\" , round (median(lead_hours), 1 )) print ( \"change failure rate:\" , sum (d[ \"failed\" ] for d in deploys) / len (deploys)) The SPACE framework Researchers from Microsoft, GitHub and the University of Victoria proposed SPACE as a way to think about developer productivity beyond one number. It covers five dimensions: Satisfaction and well-being : how developers feel about their work and tools Performance : outcomes such as quality and impact Activity : counts of actions such as commits or deploys Communication and collaboration : how well people and teams work together Efficiency and flow : how smoothly work proceeds with few interruptions and waits The core advice is to measure across several dimensions, and to include at least one perception measure. Activity counts alone, such as lines of code or number of commits, are easy to collect and easy to game. Cognitive load and onboarding Two measures speak directly to what a platform does. Time to first deploy for a new engineer, from laptop setup to a change running in a real environment, shows how much the platform removes. Time to create a new service shows the cost of starting something. Track both as medians, and run a short survey after each task asking how hard it was and what slowed the person down. A useful survey is brief and recurring: how easy is it to deploy, to debug in production, to find documentation, to get help? Add a free-text question. A rating alone tells you that something is wrong; the comment tells you what. Treat adoption as evidence, not proof Rising usage of the platform is good news but not proof of value, especially if it is mandatory. Pair it with satisfaction and outcome measures. If usage is high and sa"},"platform/platform-slos-support-and-on-call-for-the-platform-team":{"html":"\u003cp>When the pipeline is down, every team stops shipping. When the cluster API is slow, every deploy fails. The platform is a dependency of everything else, which makes its reliability matter more than that of any single product. Treat it like a service with customers, objectives, an error budget and an on-call rotation.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Which platform capabilities need SLOs, and what to measure\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to design support so it does not drown the team\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What the platform team owns versus what product teams own\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to communicate incidents and changes\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"slos-for-platform-capabilities\">SLOs for platform capabilities\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pick the capabilities users depend on most and describe their reliability in terms users feel:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"table-wrap\" tabindex=\"0\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Table\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\n\u003cth>Capability\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example indicator\u003c/th>\n\u003cth>Example objective\u003c/th>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Pipeline\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Share of builds that start within 2 minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>99 percent over 30 days\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Deploy\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Share of deploys that succeed without platform error\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>99.5 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Cluster API\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Share of requests under 1 second\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>99.9 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Self-service workflows\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Time from request to ready for common tasks\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>95 percent under 15 minutes\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003ctr>\n\u003ctd>Portal\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>Availability of the catalog and templates\u003c/td>\n\u003ctd>99.5 percent\u003c/td>\n\u003c/tr>\n\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Choose indicators that exclude user errors, such as a build failing because of a user&#39;s code, and include platform faults. Set objectives with the users, based on how much downtime they can tolerate. Do not aim for the highest number you can imagine, because each extra nine costs a great deal.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"error-budgets-for-the-platform\">Error budgets for the platform\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>An error budget is the amount of unreliability the objective allows. If the pipeline objective is 99 percent of builds starting within two minutes over thirty days, the budget is one percent. When the budget is nearly spent, the platform team slows feature work and fixes reliability. When the budget is healthy, it can ship changes faster. This gives platform decisions the same discipline that the SRE practice gives to products.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"window_minutes = 30 * 24 * 60\nobjective = 0.995\nbudget_minutes = window_minutes * (1 - objective)\nbad_minutes = 85\n\nremaining = budget_minutes - bad_minutes\nprint(&quot;budget (min):&quot;, round(budget_minutes))\nprint(&quot;used (min):&quot;, bad_minutes)\nprint(&quot;remaining (min):&quot;, round(remaining))\nprint(&quot;status:&quot;, &quot;slow down changes&quot; if remaining &lt; budget_minutes * 0.25 else &quot;ok to ship&quot;)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">window_minutes = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">30\u003c/span> * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">24\u003c/span> * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">60\u003c/span>\nobjective = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.995\u003c/span>\nbudget_minutes = window_minutes * (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> - objective)\nbad_minutes = \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">85\u003c/span>\n\nremaining = budget_minutes - bad_minutes\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;budget (min):&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">round\u003c/span>(budget_minutes))\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;used (min):&quot;\u003c/span>, bad_minutes)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;remaining (min):&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">round\u003c/span>(remaining))\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status:&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;slow down changes&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> remaining &lt; budget_minutes * \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.25\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ok to ship&quot;\u003c/span>)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"a-support-model-that-scales\">A support model that scales\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Without a plan, support consumes the whole team. A workable structure has layers:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Self-service documentation\u003c/strong> that answers the common questions, kept current from the questions actually asked\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>A request channel or form\u003c/strong> with templates, so each question arrives with the information needed\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>A rotating support engineer\u003c/strong> who handles incoming questions for a week, so the rest of the team can focus\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Escalation to the owning engineers\u003c/strong> for real defects\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Categorise questions. If the same question appears ten times, fix the cause: improve the template, error message or documentation. The goal is a falling number of questions per user.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"who-is-on-call-for-what\">Who is on call for what\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write a clear boundary. The platform team is on call for the platform&#39;s own components: the pipeline system, the cluster control plane, the portal, shared databases, and the workflows. Product teams are on call for their services, including those that run on the platform. A service failing because of its own code is not a platform incident, even though it runs on the platform&#39;s cluster. Make this boundary visible, with example scenarios, so nobody wastes the first twenty minutes arguing about whose problem it is.\u003c/p>\n\u003cp>Give product teams good tools to diagnose their own services, such as default dashboards and logs, so they can tell whether the problem is theirs or the platform&#39;s.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"communicate-well\">Communicate well\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Maintain a status page for the platform. During incidents, post updates at a regular cadence. Announce planned maintenance and upgrades in advance, with a date, the expected impact and what users must do. After a significant incident, publish a short blameless review. People forgive outages they understand and hate surprises.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"plan-upgrades-as-a-service\">Plan upgrades as a service\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The platform depends on clusters, controllers and tools that need upgrades. Give each a schedule, test upgrades in a non-production copy, and use staged rollouts. Tell teams what changes for them, and provide automated fixes where possible.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"slo&gt;bud;bud&gt;sup;bud&gt;oc;sup&gt;com;oc&gt;com\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Running the platform as a service\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"12.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"37.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"37.5\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"62.5\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"62.5\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"87.5\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"slo\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:12.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">SLOs\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"bud\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:37.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Error budget\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"sup\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:16.7%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Support model\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"oc\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:62.5%;top:83.3%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Platform on call\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"com\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:87.5%;top:50.0%;--w:22.0%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Status and notes\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, SLOs to Error budget; 2, Error budget to Support model; 3, Error budget to Platform on call; 4, Support model to Status and notes; 5, Platform on call to Status and notes.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"slo\">\u003cdt>SLOs \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Targets for pipeline, deploy and workflows.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Targets set without users.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"bud\">\u003cdt>Error budget \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Decides pace of change versus reliability.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Ignored when spent.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"sup\">\u003cdt>Support model \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Docs, forms, rotation and escalation.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> One person answers everything.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"oc\">\u003cdt>Platform on call \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Owns platform components.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Unclear boundary with product teams.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"com\">\u003cdt>Status and notes \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Status page, notices and reviews.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Silence during incidents.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-whose-problem-is-it\">Incident: Whose problem is it?\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>At 11:40 several teams reported failing deploys. The platform on-call spent half an hour on a pipeline that was healthy, while a different cause was fixing itself.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"platformctl status pipeline\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>platformctl status pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">build queue:      healthy (median wait 41s)\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">deploy workers:   healthy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">registry:         degraded: rate limit from upstream\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The failure was a rate limit on pulls from a public registry, which showed up as deploy failures. The team added the registry to the platform&#39;s own indicators, put a pull-through cache in front of it, and updated the status page template to show dependencies on outside services.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Setting an SLO nobody asked for and nobody measures\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring the error budget when it is spent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting every question go straight to senior engineers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving the on-call boundary between platform and products vague\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Going silent during platform incidents\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"slos-for-platform-capabilities","text":"SLOs for platform capabilities"},{"id":"error-budgets-for-the-platform","text":"Error budgets for the platform"},{"id":"a-support-model-that-scales","text":"A support model that scales"},{"id":"who-is-on-call-for-what","text":"Who is on call for what"},{"id":"communicate-well","text":"Communicate well"},{"id":"plan-upgrades-as-a-service","text":"Plan upgrades as a service"},{"id":"incident-whose-problem-is-it","text":"Incident: Whose problem is it?"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Google SRE book: service level objectives","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/service-level-objectives/"},{"t":"Google SRE workbook: implementing SLOs","u":"https://sre.google/workbook/implementing-slos/"},{"t":"Google SRE book: being on-call","u":"https://sre.google/sre-book/being-on-call/"}],"text":"When the pipeline is down, every team stops shipping. When the cluster API is slow, every deploy fails. The platform is a dependency of everything else, which makes its reliability matter more than that of any single product. Treat it like a service with customers, objectives, an error budget and an on-call rotation. What you will learn Which platform capabilities need SLOs, and what to measure How to design support so it does not drown the team What the platform team owns versus what product teams own How to communicate incidents and changes SLOs for platform capabilities Pick the capabilities users depend on most and describe their reliability in terms users feel: Capability Example indicator Example objective Pipeline Share of builds that start within 2 minutes 99 percent over 30 days Deploy Share of deploys that succeed without platform error 99.5 percent Cluster API Share of requests under 1 second 99.9 percent Self-service workflows Time from request to ready for common tasks 95 percent under 15 minutes Portal Availability of the catalog and templates 99.5 percent Choose indicators that exclude user errors, such as a build failing because of a user&#39;s code, and include platform faults. Set objectives with the users, based on how much downtime they can tolerate. Do not aim for the highest number you can imagine, because each extra nine costs a great deal. Error budgets for the platform An error budget is the amount of unreliability the objective allows. If the pipeline objective is 99 percent of builds starting within two minutes over thirty days, the budget is one percent. When the budget is nearly spent, the platform team slows feature work and fixes reliability. When the budget is healthy, it can ship changes faster. This gives platform decisions the same discipline that the SRE practice gives to products. window_minutes = 30 * 24 * 60 objective = 0.995 budget_minutes = window_minutes * ( 1 - objective) bad_minutes = 85 remaining = budget_minutes - bad_minutes print ( \"budget (min):\" , round (budget_minutes)) print ( \"used (min):\" , bad_minutes) print ( \"remaining (min):\" , round (remaining)) print ( \"status:\" , \"slow down changes\" if remaining \u003c budget_minutes * 0.25 else \"ok to ship\" ) A support model that scales Without a plan, support consumes the whole team. A workable structure has layers: Self-service documentation that answers the common questions, kept current from the questions actually asked A request channel or form with templates, so each question arrives with the information needed A rotating support engineer who handles incoming questions for a week, so the rest of the team can focus Escalation to the owning engineers for real defects Categorise questions. If the same question appears ten times, fix the cause: improve the template, error message or documentation. The goal is a falling number of questions per user. Who is on call for what Write a clear boundary. The platform team is on call for the platform&#39;s own components: the pipeline system, the cluster control plane, the portal, shared databases, and the workflows. Product teams are on call for their services, including those that run on the platform. A service failing because of its own code is not a platform incident, even though it runs on the platform&#39;s cluster. Make this boundary visible, with example scenarios, so nobody wastes the first twenty minutes arguing about whose problem it is. Give product teams good tools to diagnose their own services, such as default dashboards and logs, so they can tell whether the problem is theirs or the platform&#39;s. Communicate well Maintain a status page for the platform. During incidents, post updates at a regular cadence. Announce planned maintenance and upgrades in advance, with a date, the expected impact and what users must do. After a significant incident, publish a short blameless review. People forgive outages they understand and hate surprises. Plan upgrades as a service The platform dep"},"platform/adoption-migration-and-deprecation":{"html":"\u003cp>Building a platform is half of the work. The other half is getting teams from where they are to where the platform is, and then switching off the old ways. Companies that skip this end up paying for both worlds: the new platform and the old pipelines, scripts and servers that nobody dared remove.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How to plan adoption with pilots and waves\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What a migration kit contains\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to deprecate responsibly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to deal with teams that cannot or will not move\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"start-with-a-pilot\">Start with a pilot\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Pick two or three teams that have a real need, a friendly attitude and a service that matters but is not the most critical. Work with them closely, fix what breaks, and record how long each step took. Their results become the story and the checklist for everyone else. Avoid starting with the most critical system, where any bug is expensive, or with the least engaged team, which teaches you little.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"migrate-in-waves\">Migrate in waves\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>After the pilot, group the remaining services by effort and risk. Early waves take services that match the golden path well. Later waves take the awkward ones: unusual runtimes, stateful systems, regulated workloads. For each wave, schedule dates, name a migration contact on each team and assign a platform engineer who can help. A wave plan with dates turns &quot;we should move&quot; into a commitment.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-migration-kit\">The migration kit\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Make moving cheap. A kit usually includes:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>An inventory of what each service uses today, found by scanning repositories and pipelines\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A step-by-step guide with an estimate of effort\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Automation that opens a pull request adding the new pipeline and catalog descriptor\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A checklist of verification steps to compare old and new behaviour\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A rollback plan so teams are not afraid to try\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Offer office hours or embedded help for the first weeks. The point is to reduce the effort a team needs to put in.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"track-adoption-honestly\">Track adoption honestly\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Track the share of services on the platform, by tier and by team, as well as how recently they were updated. Also track the reverse: who moved and then left again, and why. The snippet below summarises a migration list by wave.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"services = [\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;checkout&quot;, &quot;wave&quot;: 1, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;done&quot;},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;search&quot;, &quot;wave&quot;: 1, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;done&quot;},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;billing&quot;, &quot;wave&quot;: 2, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;in progress&quot;},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;reports&quot;, &quot;wave&quot;: 2, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;blocked&quot;},\n    {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;mainframe-bridge&quot;, &quot;wave&quot;: 3, &quot;status&quot;: &quot;not started&quot;},\n]\n\nwaves = {}\nfor s in services:\n    w = waves.setdefault(s[&quot;wave&quot;], {&quot;total&quot;: 0, &quot;done&quot;: 0})\n    w[&quot;total&quot;] += 1\n    w[&quot;done&quot;] += s[&quot;status&quot;] == &quot;done&quot;\nfor wave, w in sorted(waves.items()):\n    print(f&quot;wave {wave}: {w['done']}/{w['total']} done&quot;)\nprint(&quot;blocked:&quot;, [s[&quot;name&quot;] for s in services if s[&quot;status&quot;] == &quot;blocked&quot;])\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">services = [\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;checkout&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;wave&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;done&quot;\u003c/span>},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;search&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;wave&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;done&quot;\u003c/span>},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;billing&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;wave&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;in progress&quot;\u003c/span>},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;reports&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;wave&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;blocked&quot;\u003c/span>},\n    {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;mainframe-bridge&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;wave&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;not started&quot;\u003c/span>},\n]\n\nwaves = {}\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> s \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> services:\n    w = waves.setdefault(s[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;wave&quot;\u003c/span>], {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;total&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;done&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>})\n    w[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;total&quot;\u003c/span>] += \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>\n    w[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;done&quot;\u003c/span>] += s[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;\u003c/span>] == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;done&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> wave, w \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sorted\u003c/span>(waves.items()):\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;wave \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{wave}\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{w[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;done&#x27;\u003c/span>]}\u003c/span>/\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{w[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&#x27;total&#x27;\u003c/span>]}\u003c/span> done&quot;\u003c/span>)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;blocked:&quot;\u003c/span>, [s[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>] \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> s \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> services \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> s[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;status&quot;\u003c/span>] == \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;blocked&quot;\u003c/span>])\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"deprecate-with-dates\">Deprecate with dates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Deprecation is a promise with a schedule. Announce what will be retired, why, when and what to do instead. A typical sequence is:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Announce with the date and the migration guide\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Stop creating new things on the old path\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Warn when the old path is used, with a link to the guide\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Remove support and switch the old path off on the announced date\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003cp>Stick to the date, but build in a clear process for exceptions with owners and expiry. If dates always slip, nobody believes the next one.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"handle-the-teams-that-stay\">Handle the teams that stay\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Some teams have good reasons to stay: a system near end of life, a regulatory constraint or an unusual need the platform does not meet. Record the reason. If several teams share it, the platform has a gap worth closing. If a team simply does not want to move, escalate with data on risk, cost and support burden, and agree an end date with its leadership. Do not let a &quot;temporary&quot; exception become permanent.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"keep-the-old-and-the-new-apart\">Keep the old and the new apart\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>During migration, avoid a half-state where a service is partly on the old path and partly on the new. Define a clear cutover: traffic shifts, the old pipeline is disabled and the old resources are cleaned up. Leave nothing running &quot;just in case&quot; without an owner and a removal date.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"measure-the-end-state\">Measure the end state\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A migration is done when the old system is gone, not when the new one is available. Track the decommission: old pipelines deleted, old servers turned off, old credentials revoked and old documentation archived. Each retired system reduces cost, risk and confusion.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"pilot&gt;kit;kit&gt;w1;kit&gt;w2;w1&gt;dep;w2&gt;dep;dep&gt;done\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">Adoption in waves\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"83.3\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-external\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pilot\" data-kind=\"external\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Pilot teams\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"kit\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Migration kit\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"w1\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Wave one\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"w2\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Later waves\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"dep\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Deprecation\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"done\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Old system removed\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Pilot teams to Migration kit; 2, Migration kit to Wave one; 3, Migration kit to Later waves; 4, Wave one to Deprecation; 5, Later waves to Deprecation; 6, Deprecation to Old system removed.\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-external\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Outside party\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pilot\">\u003cdt>Pilot teams \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Outside party\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Two or three willing teams.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Starting with the hardest system.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"kit\">\u003cdt>Migration kit \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Inventory, guide, automation and checks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Manual steps nobody wrote down.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"w1\">\u003cdt>Wave one \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Services that fit the path well.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No dates or contacts.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"w2\">\u003cdt>Later waves \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Awkward and stateful services.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Postponed forever.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"dep\">\u003cdt>Deprecation \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Dates, warnings and removal.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Dates that always slip.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"done\">\u003cdt>Old system removed \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pipelines, servers and credentials gone.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Left running just in case.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-old-pipeline-that-was-still-deploying\">Incident: The old pipeline that was still deploying\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Six months after the new platform launched, a security fix reached only part of the fleet, because ten services still deployed through a pipeline that nobody maintained.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"platformctl audit pipelines --legacy\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>platformctl audit pipelines --legacy\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">legacy pipelines still active: 10\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">last owner change: 14 months ago\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">credentials rotated: never\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The old pipeline had never been listed as something to retire. The platform team inventoried it, announced a removal date, opened pull requests moving the ten services and revoked the old credentials on the date. They added &quot;decommission&quot; as a tracked item to every future migration.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Starting with the most critical or the least willing team\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Offering the platform without migration tooling\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Announcing deprecation with no date\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Allowing temporary exceptions to become permanent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Declaring victory before the old system is removed\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"start-with-a-pilot","text":"Start with a pilot"},{"id":"migrate-in-waves","text":"Migrate in waves"},{"id":"the-migration-kit","text":"The migration kit"},{"id":"track-adoption-honestly","text":"Track adoption honestly"},{"id":"deprecate-with-dates","text":"Deprecate with dates"},{"id":"handle-the-teams-that-stay","text":"Handle the teams that stay"},{"id":"keep-the-old-and-the-new-apart","text":"Keep the old and the new apart"},{"id":"measure-the-end-state","text":"Measure the end state"},{"id":"incident-the-old-pipeline-that-was-still-deploying","text":"Incident: The old pipeline that was still deploying"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"CNCF platforms white paper","u":"https://tag-app-delivery.cncf.io/whitepapers/platforms/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: deprecation policy","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/reference/using-api/deprecation-policy/"}],"text":"Building a platform is half of the work. The other half is getting teams from where they are to where the platform is, and then switching off the old ways. Companies that skip this end up paying for both worlds: the new platform and the old pipelines, scripts and servers that nobody dared remove. What you will learn How to plan adoption with pilots and waves What a migration kit contains How to deprecate responsibly How to deal with teams that cannot or will not move Start with a pilot Pick two or three teams that have a real need, a friendly attitude and a service that matters but is not the most critical. Work with them closely, fix what breaks, and record how long each step took. Their results become the story and the checklist for everyone else. Avoid starting with the most critical system, where any bug is expensive, or with the least engaged team, which teaches you little. Migrate in waves After the pilot, group the remaining services by effort and risk. Early waves take services that match the golden path well. Later waves take the awkward ones: unusual runtimes, stateful systems, regulated workloads. For each wave, schedule dates, name a migration contact on each team and assign a platform engineer who can help. A wave plan with dates turns \"we should move\" into a commitment. The migration kit Make moving cheap. A kit usually includes: An inventory of what each service uses today, found by scanning repositories and pipelines A step-by-step guide with an estimate of effort Automation that opens a pull request adding the new pipeline and catalog descriptor A checklist of verification steps to compare old and new behaviour A rollback plan so teams are not afraid to try Offer office hours or embedded help for the first weeks. The point is to reduce the effort a team needs to put in. Track adoption honestly Track the share of services on the platform, by tier and by team, as well as how recently they were updated. Also track the reverse: who moved and then left again, and why. The snippet below summarises a migration list by wave. services = [ { \"name\" : \"checkout\" , \"wave\" : 1 , \"status\" : \"done\" }, { \"name\" : \"search\" , \"wave\" : 1 , \"status\" : \"done\" }, { \"name\" : \"billing\" , \"wave\" : 2 , \"status\" : \"in progress\" }, { \"name\" : \"reports\" , \"wave\" : 2 , \"status\" : \"blocked\" }, { \"name\" : \"mainframe-bridge\" , \"wave\" : 3 , \"status\" : \"not started\" }, ] waves = {} for s in services: w = waves.setdefault(s[ \"wave\" ], { \"total\" : 0 , \"done\" : 0 }) w[ \"total\" ] += 1 w[ \"done\" ] += s[ \"status\" ] == \"done\" for wave, w in sorted (waves.items()): print ( f\"wave {wave} : {w[ &#x27;done&#x27; ]} / {w[ &#x27;total&#x27; ]} done\" ) print ( \"blocked:\" , [s[ \"name\" ] for s in services if s[ \"status\" ] == \"blocked\" ]) Deprecate with dates Deprecation is a promise with a schedule. Announce what will be retired, why, when and what to do instead. A typical sequence is: Announce with the date and the migration guide Stop creating new things on the old path Warn when the old path is used, with a link to the guide Remove support and switch the old path off on the announced date Stick to the date, but build in a clear process for exceptions with owners and expiry. If dates always slip, nobody believes the next one. Handle the teams that stay Some teams have good reasons to stay: a system near end of life, a regulatory constraint or an unusual need the platform does not meet. Record the reason. If several teams share it, the platform has a gap worth closing. If a team simply does not want to move, escalate with data on risk, cost and support burden, and agree an end date with its leadership. Do not let a \"temporary\" exception become permanent. Keep the old and the new apart During migration, avoid a half-state where a service is partly on the old path and partly on the new. Define a clear cutover: traffic shifts, the old pipeline is disabled and the old resources are cleaned up. Leave nothing running \"just in case\" without an owner and a removal da"},"platform/capstone-design-and-ship-a-golden-path":{"html":"\u003cp>This lesson is a design and build exercise. You will specify one golden path end to end, write a small scaffolder that validates a request and generates the files, check the result against policy, and plan how you would roll the path out and know it works. The code is a toy, but each part is a miniature of something a real platform does.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>How to turn a path specification into automation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to generate a catalog entry and a manifest from one request\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to check the output against policy before it is created\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>How to plan adoption and measures for the result\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"step-1-write-the-path-spec\">Step 1: Write the path spec\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write one page that answers these questions for the path &quot;web service&quot;:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Who is it for?\u003c/strong> Product teams building stateless HTTP services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>What do they get?\u003c/strong> Repository, pipeline, deployment manifest, catalog entry, dashboard and alerts\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>What is fixed?\u003c/strong> Base image, security checks, labels, resource limits\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>What can they change?\u003c/strong> Name, team, tier, replicas within a range, extra environment variables\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>What does support look like on and off the path?\u003c/strong>\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>What does success look like?\u003c/strong> Time to first deploy under an hour and 90 percent of new services created through the path within two quarters\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"step-2-build-the-scaffolder\">Step 2: Build the scaffolder\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The script below validates a request, produces a catalog entry and a deployment manifest as Python structures, and runs the policy checks from earlier lessons. Real systems would write files and call APIs. Here you can read the logic in a few lines.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"python\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"import json\nimport re\n\nTEAMS = {&quot;payments&quot;, &quot;search&quot;, &quot;platform&quot;}\nTIERS = {&quot;critical&quot;: (3, 10), &quot;standard&quot;: (2, 6), &quot;experimental&quot;: (1, 2)}\n\ndef validate(req):\n    errors = []\n    if not re.fullmatch(r&quot;[a-z][a-z0-9-]{2,29}&quot;, req.get(&quot;name&quot;, &quot;&quot;)):\n        errors.append(&quot;name must be 3-30 lowercase letters, digits or hyphens&quot;)\n    if req.get(&quot;team&quot;) not in TEAMS:\n        errors.append(&quot;unknown team&quot;)\n    tier = req.get(&quot;tier&quot;)\n    if tier not in TIERS:\n        errors.append(&quot;tier must be one of &quot; + &quot;, &quot;.join(sorted(TIERS)))\n    else:\n        lo, hi = TIERS[tier]\n        if not lo &lt;= req.get(&quot;replicas&quot;, lo) &lt;= hi:\n            errors.append(f&quot;replicas for {tier} must be {lo}-{hi}&quot;)\n    return errors\n\ndef render(req):\n    catalog = {\n        &quot;kind&quot;: &quot;Component&quot;, &quot;name&quot;: req[&quot;name&quot;], &quot;owner&quot;: &quot;team-&quot; + req[&quot;team&quot;],\n        &quot;lifecycle&quot;: &quot;production&quot; if req[&quot;tier&quot;] != &quot;experimental&quot; else &quot;experimental&quot;,\n        &quot;tier&quot;: req[&quot;tier&quot;], &quot;links&quot;: [&quot;dashboard/&quot; + req[&quot;name&quot;], &quot;runbook/&quot; + req[&quot;name&quot;]],\n    }\n    deploy = {\n        &quot;name&quot;: req[&quot;name&quot;], &quot;replicas&quot;: req.get(&quot;replicas&quot;, TIERS[req[&quot;tier&quot;]][0]),\n        &quot;labels&quot;: {&quot;owner&quot;: &quot;team-&quot; + req[&quot;team&quot;], &quot;tier&quot;: req[&quot;tier&quot;]},\n        &quot;containers&quot;: [{&quot;name&quot;: &quot;app&quot;, &quot;image&quot;: &quot;registry.example/&quot; + req[&quot;name&quot;] + &quot;:latest&quot;,\n                         &quot;resources&quot;: {&quot;limits&quot;: {&quot;memory&quot;: &quot;512Mi&quot;, &quot;cpu&quot;: &quot;500m&quot;}}, &quot;run_as_root&quot;: False}],\n    }\n    return catalog, deploy\n\ndef policy(deploy):\n    out = []\n    for c in deploy[&quot;containers&quot;]:\n        if c[&quot;image&quot;].endswith(&quot;:latest&quot;):\n            out.append(&quot;image tag latest is not allowed: pin a version&quot;)\n        if &quot;limits&quot; not in c[&quot;resources&quot;]:\n            out.append(&quot;missing resource limits&quot;)\n        if c[&quot;run_as_root&quot;]:\n            out.append(&quot;must not run as root&quot;)\n    if &quot;owner&quot; not in deploy[&quot;labels&quot;]:\n        out.append(&quot;missing owner label&quot;)\n    return out\n\nreq = {&quot;name&quot;: &quot;billing&quot;, &quot;team&quot;: &quot;payments&quot;, &quot;tier&quot;: &quot;standard&quot;, &quot;replicas&quot;: 3}\nerrs = validate(req)\nif errs:\n    print(&quot;REJECTED:&quot;, errs)\nelse:\n    catalog, deploy = render(req)\n    problems = policy(deploy)\n    print(json.dumps({&quot;catalog&quot;: catalog[&quot;name&quot;], &quot;owner&quot;: catalog[&quot;owner&quot;], &quot;policy_problems&quot;: problems}, indent=2))\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> json\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">import\u003c/span> re\n\nTEAMS = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;payments&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;search&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;platform&quot;\u003c/span>}\nTIERS = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;critical&quot;\u003c/span>: (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">10\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;standard&quot;\u003c/span>: (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">6\u003c/span>), \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;experimental&quot;\u003c/span>: (\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>)}\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">validate\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">req\u003c/span>):\n    errors = []\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> re.fullmatch(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">r&quot;[a-z][a-z0-9-]{2,29}&quot;\u003c/span>, req.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>)):\n        errors.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name must be 3-30 lowercase letters, digits or hyphens&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> req.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team&quot;\u003c/span>) \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> TEAMS:\n        errors.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;unknown team&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    tier = req.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> tier \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> TIERS:\n        errors.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier must be one of &quot;\u003c/span> + \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;, &quot;\u003c/span>.join(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">sorted\u003c/span>(TIERS)))\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span>:\n        lo, hi = TIERS[tier]\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> lo &lt;= req.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;replicas&quot;\u003c/span>, lo) &lt;= hi:\n            errors.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">f&quot;replicas for \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{tier}\u003c/span> must be \u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{lo}\u003c/span>-\u003cspan class=\"hljs-subst\">{hi}\u003c/span>&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> errors\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">render\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">req\u003c/span>):\n    catalog = {\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;kind&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;Component&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: req[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team-&quot;\u003c/span> + req[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team&quot;\u003c/span>],\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;lifecycle&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;production&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> req[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier&quot;\u003c/span>] != \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;experimental&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;experimental&quot;\u003c/span>,\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier&quot;\u003c/span>: req[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;links&quot;\u003c/span>: [\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;dashboard/&quot;\u003c/span> + req[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;runbook/&quot;\u003c/span> + req[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>]],\n    }\n    deploy = {\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: req[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;replicas&quot;\u003c/span>: req.get(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;replicas&quot;\u003c/span>, TIERS[req[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier&quot;\u003c/span>]][\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0\u003c/span>]),\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team-&quot;\u003c/span> + req[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier&quot;\u003c/span>: req[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier&quot;\u003c/span>]},\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;containers&quot;\u003c/span>: [{\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;app&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;image&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;registry.example/&quot;\u003c/span> + req[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>] + \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;:latest&quot;\u003c/span>,\n                         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;resources&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;limits&quot;\u003c/span>: {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;memory&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;512Mi&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;cpu&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;500m&quot;\u003c/span>}}, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;run_as_root&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">False\u003c/span>}],\n    }\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> catalog, deploy\n\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">def\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-title function_\">policy\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-params\">deploy\u003c/span>):\n    out = []\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">for\u003c/span> c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> deploy[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;containers&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> c[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;image&quot;\u003c/span>].endswith(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;:latest&quot;\u003c/span>):\n            out.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;image tag latest is not allowed: pin a version&quot;\u003c/span>)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;limits&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> c[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;resources&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n            out.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;missing resource limits&quot;\u003c/span>)\n        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> c[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;run_as_root&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n            out.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;must not run as root&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">not\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">in\u003c/span> deploy[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;labels&quot;\u003c/span>]:\n        out.append(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;missing owner label&quot;\u003c/span>)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">return\u003c/span> out\n\nreq = {\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;billing&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;team&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;payments&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;tier&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;standard&quot;\u003c/span>, \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;replicas&quot;\u003c/span>: \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">3\u003c/span>}\nerrs = validate(req)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">if\u003c/span> errs:\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;REJECTED:&quot;\u003c/span>, errs)\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-keyword\">else\u003c/span>:\n    catalog, deploy = render(req)\n    problems = policy(deploy)\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">print\u003c/span>(json.dumps({\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;catalog&quot;\u003c/span>: catalog[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;name&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>: catalog[\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;owner&quot;\u003c/span>], \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;policy_problems&quot;\u003c/span>: problems}, indent=\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>))\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Run it. The generated manifest uses a \u003ccode>latest\u003c/code> tag, so the policy check reports a problem. This is deliberate: the template has a bug, and the policy check is what finds it before a service is created. Fix the template to use a pinned version, rerun and confirm the problem list is empty.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"step-3-extend-it\">Step 3: Extend it\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Try these extensions, one at a time:\u003c/p>\n\u003col>\n\u003cli>Reject a request whose replica count falls outside the tier&#39;s range, and test it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add a check that rejects names already in an existing-services list.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add a \u003ccode>deprecation\u003c/code> field to the catalog entry that the path can set when a path version is retired.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Add a function that takes a list of existing services and reports which are missing owner, dashboard or runbook entries.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Write three tests: a good request, a bad name and an out-of-range replica count.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ol>\n\u003ch2 id=\"step-4-plan-the-rollout\">Step 4: Plan the rollout\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write down a short plan:\u003c/p>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Pilot:\u003c/strong> which two teams, with what success criteria\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Waves:\u003c/strong> how you will group the remaining services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Migration kit:\u003c/strong> what you will offer so moving is cheap\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Measures:\u003c/strong> time to first deploy, share of new services on the path, change failure rate and a developer satisfaction question\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Support:\u003c/strong> who answers questions during the first month\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Deprecation:\u003c/strong> what old route you will retire and by when\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-to-check\">What to check\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A good capstone has a one-page spec, a scaffolder that refuses bad input with clear messages, a policy check that catches an unsafe default, tests, and a rollout plan with dates and measures. Ask a colleague to try your path as a new user and time how long it takes them.\u003c/p>\n\u003cfigure class=\"dg\" data-rows=\"3\" data-edges=\"spec&gt;req;req&gt;val;val&gt;gen;gen&gt;pol;spec&gt;roll;pol&gt;roll&gt;feedback\" style=\"--rows:3\">\u003cfigcaption class=\"dg-title\">The capstone end to end\u003c/figcaption>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-stage\">\u003csvg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\" preserveAspectRatio=\"none\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\">\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"30.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"30.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"16.7\"/>\u003cline x1=\"50.0\" y1=\"16.7\" x2=\"70.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"70.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"90.0\" y2=\"50.0\"/>\u003cline x1=\"10.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003cline x1=\"90.0\" y1=\"50.0\" x2=\"50.0\" y2=\"83.3\"/>\u003c/svg>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-store\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"spec\" data-kind=\"store\" style=\"left:10.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Path specification\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-service\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"req\" data-kind=\"service\" style=\"left:30.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Request\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"val\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:16.7%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Validate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-process\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"gen\" data-kind=\"process\" style=\"left:70.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Generate\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-security\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"pol\" data-kind=\"security\" style=\"left:90.0%;top:50.0%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Policy check\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"dg-node k-observe\" data-act=\"dg-pick\" data-id=\"roll\" data-kind=\"observe\" style=\"left:50.0%;top:83.3%;--w:17.6%\" aria-pressed=\"false\">\u003cspan class=\"dg-name\">Rollout plan\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"dg-kind\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/button>\u003c/div>\u003cp class=\"sr\">Flow in order: 1, Path specification to Request; 2, Request to Validate; 3, Validate to Generate; 4, Generate to Policy check; 5, Path specification to Rollout plan; 6, Policy check to Rollout plan (feedback).\u003c/p>\u003cul class=\"dg-legend\" aria-label=\"Legend\">\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-store\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Data store\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-service\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Service\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-security\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Security\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-process\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Step\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-sw k-observe\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Observability\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci class=\"dg-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">\u003c/i>Numbered arrows show the order of the flow\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp class=\"dg-hint small\">Select a component to see what it does and what happens when it fails. \u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-quiet dg-flow-btn\" data-act=\"dg-flow\" aria-pressed=\"false\">Show the flow\u003c/button>\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-live\" role=\"status\" aria-live=\"polite\">\u003c/div>\u003cdl class=\"dg-list\">\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"spec\">\u003cdt>Path specification \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Data store\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Who, what, fixed and flexible, success.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> A spec nobody reads.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"req\">\u003cdt>Request \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Service\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Name, team, tier and replicas.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Too many questions.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"val\">\u003cdt>Validate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Naming, team, tier and range checks.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Checks after creation.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"gen\">\u003cdt>Generate \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Step\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Catalog entry and manifest.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Template with unsafe defaults.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"pol\">\u003cdt>Policy check \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Security\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Image tag, limits, root and owner.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> Policy not run in the template.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"dg-item\" data-id=\"roll\">\u003cdt>Rollout plan \u003cspan class=\"dg-kt\">Observability\u003c/span>\u003c/dt>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">What it does:\u003c/span> Pilot, waves, kit and measures.\u003c/dd>\u003cdd>\u003cspan class=\"dg-k\">If it fails or is weak:\u003c/span> No dates or owners.\u003c/dd>\u003c/div>\u003c/dl>\u003c/figure>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-template-that-shipped-latest\">Incident: The template that shipped latest\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A golden path template used an unpinned image tag. For a month, new services deployed whatever the newest image happened to be, and one release broke three services at once.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"platformctl lint template web-service\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>platformctl lint template web-service\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FAIL image tag is latest in deploy/manifest.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">FAIL no policy check in template pipeline\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">(this is a simulated illustration, not a real incident)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The template had never been tested in CI, and its pipeline did not run the same policy as the cluster. The team added a CI job that generated a sample service and ran the full policy check, pinned the image tag in the template, and added an automatic pull request to update existing services.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Writing the template before the spec\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping tests of the template itself\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Running policy only in the cluster, not at creation time\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving no plan for existing services\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Measuring only how many services use the template, not whether they are better off\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"step-1-write-the-path-spec","text":"Step 1: Write the path spec"},{"id":"step-2-build-the-scaffolder","text":"Step 2: Build the scaffolder"},{"id":"step-3-extend-it","text":"Step 3: Extend it"},{"id":"step-4-plan-the-rollout","text":"Step 4: Plan the rollout"},{"id":"what-to-check","text":"What to check"},{"id":"incident-the-template-that-shipped-latest","text":"Incident: The template that shipped latest"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Backstage: software templates","u":"https://backstage.io/docs/features/software-templates/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: pod security standards","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/security/pod-security-standards/"}],"text":"This lesson is a design and build exercise. You will specify one golden path end to end, write a small scaffolder that validates a request and generates the files, check the result against policy, and plan how you would roll the path out and know it works. The code is a toy, but each part is a miniature of something a real platform does. What you will learn How to turn a path specification into automation How to generate a catalog entry and a manifest from one request How to check the output against policy before it is created How to plan adoption and measures for the result Step 1: Write the path spec Write one page that answers these questions for the path \"web service\": Who is it for? Product teams building stateless HTTP services What do they get? Repository, pipeline, deployment manifest, catalog entry, dashboard and alerts What is fixed? Base image, security checks, labels, resource limits What can they change? Name, team, tier, replicas within a range, extra environment variables What does support look like on and off the path? What does success look like? Time to first deploy under an hour and 90 percent of new services created through the path within two quarters Step 2: Build the scaffolder The script below validates a request, produces a catalog entry and a deployment manifest as Python structures, and runs the policy checks from earlier lessons. Real systems would write files and call APIs. Here you can read the logic in a few lines. import json import re TEAMS = { \"payments\" , \"search\" , \"platform\" } TIERS = { \"critical\" : ( 3 , 10 ), \"standard\" : ( 2 , 6 ), \"experimental\" : ( 1 , 2 )} def validate ( req ): errors = [] if not re.fullmatch( r\"[a-z][a-z0-9-]{2,29}\" , req.get( \"name\" , \"\" )): errors.append( \"name must be 3-30 lowercase letters, digits or hyphens\" ) if req.get( \"team\" ) not in TEAMS: errors.append( \"unknown team\" ) tier = req.get( \"tier\" ) if tier not in TIERS: errors.append( \"tier must be one of \" + \", \" .join( sorted (TIERS))) else : lo, hi = TIERS[tier] if not lo \u003c= req.get( \"replicas\" , lo) \u003c= hi: errors.append( f\"replicas for {tier} must be {lo} - {hi} \" ) return errors def render ( req ): catalog = { \"kind\" : \"Component\" , \"name\" : req[ \"name\" ], \"owner\" : \"team-\" + req[ \"team\" ], \"lifecycle\" : \"production\" if req[ \"tier\" ] != \"experimental\" else \"experimental\" , \"tier\" : req[ \"tier\" ], \"links\" : [ \"dashboard/\" + req[ \"name\" ], \"runbook/\" + req[ \"name\" ]], } deploy = { \"name\" : req[ \"name\" ], \"replicas\" : req.get( \"replicas\" , TIERS[req[ \"tier\" ]][ 0 ]), \"labels\" : { \"owner\" : \"team-\" + req[ \"team\" ], \"tier\" : req[ \"tier\" ]}, \"containers\" : [{ \"name\" : \"app\" , \"image\" : \"registry.example/\" + req[ \"name\" ] + \":latest\" , \"resources\" : { \"limits\" : { \"memory\" : \"512Mi\" , \"cpu\" : \"500m\" }}, \"run_as_root\" : False }], } return catalog, deploy def policy ( deploy ): out = [] for c in deploy[ \"containers\" ]: if c[ \"image\" ].endswith( \":latest\" ): out.append( \"image tag latest is not allowed: pin a version\" ) if \"limits\" not in c[ \"resources\" ]: out.append( \"missing resource limits\" ) if c[ \"run_as_root\" ]: out.append( \"must not run as root\" ) if \"owner\" not in deploy[ \"labels\" ]: out.append( \"missing owner label\" ) return out req = { \"name\" : \"billing\" , \"team\" : \"payments\" , \"tier\" : \"standard\" , \"replicas\" : 3 } errs = validate(req) if errs: print ( \"REJECTED:\" , errs) else : catalog, deploy = render(req) problems = policy(deploy) print (json.dumps({ \"catalog\" : catalog[ \"name\" ], \"owner\" : catalog[ \"owner\" ], \"policy_problems\" : problems}, indent= 2 )) Run it. The generated manifest uses a latest tag, so the policy check reports a problem. This is deliberate: the template has a bug, and the policy check is what finds it before a service is created. Fix the template to use a pinned version, rerun and confirm the problem list is empty. Step 3: Extend it Try these extensions, one at a time: Reject a request whose replica count falls outside the tier&#39;s range, and test it. Add a check that rejects nam"}});